# CLAUDE.md

Guidance for Claude Code (or any future agent) working in this repo.

## What this is

A minimal Android browser called **Yuku**: WebView engine, 100% Jetpack Compose
UI, single `MainActivity`. Package is `com.yuku.browser`; the launcher label is
`app_name` in `res/values/strings.xml` and the Gradle root project is `Yuku`. See
`README.md` for the full feature/stub inventory and layout map — don't
duplicate that here, just keep this file pointed at it.

## Building and running

No JDK or Gradle wrapper jar ships with a fresh checkout on some machines.
What has worked in this environment:

```bash
export JAVA_HOME="/Users/alex/Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/jbr-21.0.11/Contents/Home"
export ANDROID_HOME="$HOME/Library/Android/sdk"
export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools:$ANDROID_HOME/emulator:$PATH"
./gradlew :app:installDebug
```

- The Android Studio–bundled JBR (`/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr`)
  is JDK 25 on this machine — **too new** for Gradle 8.9/AGP 8.7.3. Use the separate
  JBR 21 install above instead.
- Installed package during dev is `com.yuku.browser.debug` (debug
  `applicationIdSuffix`). Launch with:
  `adb shell am start -n com.yuku.browser.debug/com.yuku.browser.MainActivity`
- Emulator used in this environment: AVD name `browser_test`.
- The Gradle daemon caches this JDK per-project — if a build ever fails with a
  JVM-version error, run the export block above in the same shell rather than
  relying on inherited env, and `./gradlew --stop` before retrying.
- **Never judge animation smoothness on the debug build.** Measured on a
  Nothing Phone (120Hz) with `dumpsys gfxinfo <pkg> framestats`: cold launch
  1945ms debug vs 862ms release; the tab switcher's UI thread 6.9ms/frame
  debug vs 3.9ms release (p99 40ms vs 12ms); the sheet's recomposition 1.66ms
  vs 0.61ms. Debug drops frames the release build does not. `debugImplementation
  ui-tooling`, unoptimised bytecode and no R8 are the difference. To measure
  honestly: `./gradlew :app:assembleRelease`, sign the unsigned APK with the
  debug keystore (`apksigner sign --ks ~/.android/debug.keystore --ks-pass
  pass:android --ks-key-alias androiddebugkey`), install it — it is
  `com.yuku.browser`, so it coexists with the `.debug` build.
- No unit/instrumented test suite exists yet — verification has been manual,
  via `adb shell input tap/swipe` + `adb exec-out screencap`, or `uiautomator
  dump` to get exact element bounds when blind-tapping coordinates gets
  unreliable (sheet entrance animations shift layout between screenshot and
  tap).

## Searching the source (shell gotchas)

The shell here is **zsh**, and the repo root is `/Users/alex/Downloads/browser`.
Two things reliably return exit code 1 for reasons that have nothing to do with
the search:

- `grep -rn foo --include=*.kt .` — zsh expands `*.kt` itself and fails with
  `no matches found`. **Quote it**: `--include='*.kt'` (or search the source
  root directly, below).
- `cd app/src/main/java/com/yuku/browser` — the Bash tool's working directory
  *persists between calls*, so a relative `cd` that worked once fails the next
  time ("no such file or directory") while the commands after it still run
  against the old directory. Use absolute paths, or prefix each call with
  `cd /Users/alex/Downloads/browser &&`.

All Kotlin lives under one path — worth using verbatim:

```bash
cd /Users/alex/Downloads/browser && grep -rn 'quickSwitch' app/src/main/java/com/yuku/browser
```

`core/` and `ui/` under that path are the two source packages; resources are in
`app/src/main/res`.

## Workflow policy: build, install, then stop

After making a code change, always build and `installDebug` to the emulator
so the new build is ready to try — but then stop and hand off, rather than
driving the UI yourself (tapping through screens, screenshotting, verifying
behavior). Default to letting the user test on-device themselves. Only
poke at the running app yourself (screenshots, `adb shell input`, etc.) when
explicitly asked to investigate or verify something specific.

## Architecture conventions to follow

- **`core/` has no Compose dependency.** `BrowserViewModel` and its data
  classes (`Tab`, `HistoryEntry`, `BookmarkEntry`, `SearchEngine`,
  `AccentTheme`, `ThemeMode`) are plain Kotlin/Android. Compose-only types
  (`Color`, etc.) live in `ui/theme/Theme.kt`, which maps the `core/` enums to
  actual colors/schemes. Keep it that way — don't import `androidx.compose.*`
  into `core/`.
- **Theming is fully dynamic, not static vals.** `Ink`, `InkStrong`, `PageBg`,
  `BarBg`, etc. in `ui/theme/Theme.kt` are `@Composable val`s backed by
  `LocalBrowserPalette` (a `CompositionLocalProvider`), not fixed constants —
  every screen that references them already reacts to light/dark/Dynamic
  without extra wiring. `AccentColor` similarly just reads
  `MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primary`. Don't reintroduce hardcoded `Color(0x…)`
  literals for chrome; add a token to `BrowserPalette` (and both `LightPalette`
  /`DarkPalette`) instead. The one deliberate exception is the private-tab
  placeholder (`Color(0xFF121212)`) — that's semantic, not chrome.
- **Full-screen "destination" pattern.** Settings, Bookmarks, and History are
  not sheets — they're `AnimatedVisibility` overlays (slide-up + fade) toggled
  by a `xOpen: Boolean` state in `BrowserScreen`, each wired into the shared
  `BackHandler` chain. Follow this same pattern for any new full-screen
  destination reached from the menu, rather than adding another
  `ModalBottomSheet` variant.
- **Sheets share one `ModalBottomSheet`.** `NewTabSheet` and `MenuSheet` are
  both rendered through a single `sheet: Sheet?` enum + `when` block in
  `BrowserScreen`, so switching between them (e.g. tapping the address bar to
  edit it) is just changing `sheet`'s value — no separate dismiss/reopen
  animation needed.
- **In-memory-only state is the norm.** Search engine, accent theme, theme
  mode, search-suggestions toggle, and bookmarks all live in
  `BrowserViewModel` as `MutableStateFlow`s with **no persistence** — they
  reset on process death. This is intentional/consistent with the rest of the
  scaffold (tabs and history are the same); don't add a persistence layer for
  one feature without doing it for all of them, and call it out if asked to
  add real persistence.
- **Icons**: Material Icons Extended, using the Filled/Outlined pair
  convention where a control has on/off state (e.g. `Bookmark`/
  `BookmarkBorder`, `DarkMode`/`DarkMode` outlined) — filled means "on".
  Prefer `Icons.AutoMirrored.*` over the deprecated non-mirrored versions for
  anything direction-sensitive (back/forward chevrons etc.).
- **Custom font resources**: `res/font/bitcount_grid_double.ttf` is the
  *variable* font file (has a live `wght` axis), not a static per-weight cut —
  fetched from the `google/fonts` GitHub repo, not the CSS API (which only
  serves discrete static instances). Render arbitrary weights via
  `FontVariation.weight(n)` (`@OptIn(ExperimentalTextApi::class)`), not by
  swapping font files.

## Recent changes (most recent first)

- **The list switcher arrives over the page instead of swallowing it**
  (`TabListSwitcher`'s `open` parameter, its own `fade` Animatable and its
  rows' staggered `enter`; `BrowserScreen`'s `pageShrinkSuppressed`, replacing
  `liveShrinkSuppressed`). The grid shrinks the page down into a card, which
  is honest there — the card IS the page, smaller. A list row is not, so
  animating a page into one was claiming a relationship that does not exist.
  In list mode the page now stays exactly where it is, at fullscreen, and the
  list fades in over it the way the startup cover leaves, with the rows
  sliding up into it from the bottom edge one after another.
  **`progress` still runs; it is only the PAGE that stops reading it.** Every
  threshold and the live/static handoff (`liveVisible` is `progressAtZero`, so
  the WebView still gives way to its thumbnail while the list is up) are still
  measured against it, and it is what keeps this switcher MOUNTED — the
  suppression is one lambda in front of `WebViewHost`,
  `AnimatedThumbnailHost` and `FloatingTabLabel`. That label is skipped
  outright here: the row draws its own name, so it was a second copy of it
  sitting over the page at fullscreen size for the length of the fade.
  **Two things had to be fixed before any of this was visible at all, and
  both are worth knowing.**
  **A draw modifier declared before a `graphicsLayer` is OUTSIDE it.**
  `grainedBackground` is `background()` + `drawWithCache`, and it sat above
  the layer in the chain — so the switcher's opaque ground was painted at full
  opacity on the frame it mounted whatever the layer's alpha said, and only
  the rows inside were fading. An opaque surface appearing in one frame is
  exactly what "no animation" looks like. (The old scale never reached the
  ground either; the page shrinking was the only motion there had ever been.)
  **And riding `progress` for the fade is a cut, not a fade.** The grid can
  do it because the card's scale and the page's are one number and must not
  disagree; here there is nothing to agree with, and `progress` carries an
  [Overshoot] easing plus a `/ MIDPOINT_PROGRESS` that clamps. Logged out of
  the draw phase on device: alpha was 0.63 within 60ms of the tap and
  saturated at 186ms — most of the fade spent in its first fifth. It is a
  plain `tween` on its own `Animatable` now (220ms in, 180ms out), which
  measures as a clean S-curve with its midpoint at ~110ms. An `Animatable`
  seeded at 0 rather than `animateFloatAsState`: a tap sets `switcherOpen` in
  the same frame this composable first appears, so a value seeded from the
  target seeds at 1 and there is no entrance at all.
  **The old drag suppression is gone with it.** `open` is `switcherOpen`,
  which a tabs-button drag does not set until release — so the list stays
  down through the drag for free, and the separate `listProgress` lambda that
  used to do that job is deleted.
  **The rows rise into it in sequence, bottom to top.** 24dp of travel and a
  22ms step — deliberately shorter than the rise, so it reads as one movement
  travelling up the column rather than a queue of separate arrivals. Capped
  at 8 steps, since everything past the top of the screen is delay nobody
  sees. The sequence is keyed on the OPEN (a `generation` counter), not on
  this composable appearing: the drag mounts the switcher on its first pixel
  of travel, so rows keyed on composition would deal themselves out behind an
  invisible surface and be sitting still by the time it faded up. Its step is
  counted from `listState.firstVisibleItemIndex` read at that moment, and
  only the rows present then animate (`sequenceLive`, read ONCE per row —
  a row reading it live would abandon its rise half way up), so a row
  scrolled to later is composed already in place. Row alpha multiplies the
  entrance by the close animation's, or a row closed during the entrance
  snaps back to full opacity for the other's duration.

- **The launcher's shortcuts point at a package that exists, and the private
  one brings the keyboard up** (`app/src/main/shortcuts-template.xml`, moved
  out of `res/xml/`; `GenerateShortcutsTask` + the `androidComponents` block in
  `app/build.gradle.kts`; `NewTabSheet`'s open effect). Both entries on the
  launcher's long press answered with "app isn't installed".
  **A shortcut's intent has to be EXPLICIT** — its action is ours alone and no
  intent-filter answers it — and there is no way to write a variable package
  name into a resource. Two things that look like they supply one and do not:
  `${applicationId}` is substituted into AndroidManifest.xml and NOTHING else,
  so in `res/xml` it reaches the launcher verbatim and the shortcut points at a
  package literally called that; and a string RESOURCE is not resolved either,
  since the framework's `ShortcutParser` reads this attribute as raw text —
  `dumpsys shortcut` showed the component as `cmp=@2131689598/…MainActivity`.
  So the file is a TEMPLATE outside `res/` and the build writes it per variant
  with the variant's own `applicationId` in it, which is also what accounts for
  the debug suffix. Note this was broken on release too, not only under
  `.debug`. `dumpsys shortcut` is how to check it: the component printed there
  is what the launcher will start.
  **And the private sheet had no keyboard, however it was reached.** The open
  effect scrolled the list to the top before asking for focus, and
  `scrollToItem` suspends until the list it belongs to has been laid out — in
  the private space there is no list at all, the history being replaced by a
  caption, so it never returned and the focus request after it was
  unreachable. The keyboard is asked for first now and the scroll is skipped
  there. Anything else queued after a `scrollToItem` in this file is exposed to
  the same trap.

- **The app is called Yuku on the device now** (`res/values/strings.xml`'s
  `app_name`, `settings.gradle.kts`'s `rootProject.name`, both `Browser`
  before). Everything else already said Yuku — the package, the overlay's
  "Open in Yuku", the passwords pane — so the launcher was the last place the
  old working title survived. Nothing in code reads `app_name`; it is the
  manifest's one `android:label`, on `<application>`, which both activities
  inherit.

- **A page asking for the camera, the mic or where you are is now a question**
  (`core/SitePermissions.kt`, new; `BrowserStore`'s `sitePermissions` /
  `Settings.permissionRules`; `BrowserViewModel`'s "site permissions" section —
  `askSitePermission` / `answerPermissionAsk` / `grantWithSystem` /
  `onOsPermissionResult`, plus `onPermissionRequest`,
  `onPermissionRequestCanceled` and `onGeolocationPermissionsShowPrompt` on the
  chrome client; `WebPlatform`'s card and `ModalCard`'s third action;
  `SettingsPane.Permissions`). Same shape as everything else in
  `WebPlatform.kt`, and for the same reason: WebView's default for all of these
  is SILENCE, not refusal — an unhandled `onPermissionRequest` never resolves,
  so `getUserMedia` hangs, and an unhandled geolocation prompt denies without
  saying so.
  **Every allow is two permissions, and keeping them apart is the whole
  shape.** The user's answer is about the SITE; whether this app may reach the
  camera at all is Android's own dialog, and it is raised only AFTER the site
  has been allowed — asking first would put a camera prompt in front of someone
  who had not yet decided to give the site a camera. `onOsPermissionResult`
  ignores what the system returned and re-checks what is actually held, which
  is the same answer for a grant and the right one for the paths that return
  nothing useful (a permission permanently denied comes straight back without a
  dialog). A permission is satisfied by ANY of its Android permissions, which
  is what makes location work for a user who granted only the coarse one.
  **Three answers, because two is the wrong number.** Allow / Allow this time /
  Block — the middle one is what the feature is for, being neither a decision
  the user has to keep making nor one they are stuck with. Session answers are
  a plain map, never persisted, and they are read BEFORE the stored ones so a
  site blocked in Settings and then allowed once is actually allowed once. A
  standing answer removes the session's, or "Block" after an earlier "Allow
  this time" would be read straight back out of the session map. A private
  tab's answers are all session answers however the button was labelled, and
  the card says so rather than relabelling its own buttons.
  **Keyed by ORIGIN, not by registrable domain**, which is the one place this
  app is stricter than its own convention (history, bookmarks and the vault are
  all keyed by domain): a permission is a capability handed to whatever is
  running on the page, and `maps.example.com` having the camera is not a reason
  for `ads.example.com` to have it. An opaque origin (a `data:` document, a
  sandboxed frame) is refused rather than asked about under a blank name, and
  the trailing slash a `PermissionRequest`'s Uri carries — which the
  geolocation callback's does not — is trimmed, or one origin would be two rows
  in Settings and two questions for the user.
  **An unrecognised resource is denied, never folded into the nearest row.**
  `grant` takes exactly the strings it is handed, so `RESOURCE_PROTECTED_MEDIA_ID`
  and MIDI fall out of `SitePermission.ofResource` as null and are refused —
  granting a resource the user was never shown is the failure the whole path
  exists to prevent. The set is closed by what WebView can RAISE, not by what
  the web platform defines: there is no Web Notifications, Bluetooth, USB or
  FedCM in WebView, so there is nothing behind a switch for any of them.
  **A cancelled request is dropped, not denied.** `onPermissionRequestCanceled`
  / `onGeolocationPermissionsHidePrompt` take the card down and throw the reply
  away: `deny()` on a withdrawn request is a call into a frame that has stopped
  listening. One question at a time, the js-dialog rule, for the same reason —
  a page can raise these in a loop.
  **The system dialog would not open at all, and the cause was a transitive
  dependency.** Every allow ended in `NotAllowedError` with the camera service
  logging a hard denial: `ActivityResultRegistry` generates request codes
  across the whole int range and hands them to `ActivityCompat.requestPermissions`,
  which asks the Activity to validate them where it implements
  `RequestPermissionsRequestCodeValidator` — and `FragmentActivity` in
  **fragment 1.2.5**, which is what `biometric:1.1.0` drags in, rejects
  anything with bits above the low 16. `IllegalArgumentException: Can only use
  lower 16 bits for requestCode`, thrown from the launcher, caught by the
  `runCatching` around it, and answered as a denial. Fragment is now pinned
  forward to 1.8.5, which dropped that validator with its own move to the
  ActivityResult APIs. The download path's storage permission on API ≤28 was
  broken the same way and by the same line — it was simply never exercised on
  a device new enough to be tested on.
  Not fixed because it is not ours: **google.com's Lens button leaves the
  browser entirely.** It resolves to the Google app, which brings its own
  camera and its own permission — the WebView is never asked, so no card is
  raised, and that is the correct outcome of "Open links in apps".
  Geolocation's `retain` argument is `false` on purpose: this app keeps the
  answer itself, where Settings can show it and take it back, and WebView's own
  per-origin store would be a second copy of the same fact that no pane can
  reach. `ModalCard` grew a `neutralLabel` and lays its actions out in a
  `FlowRow`, three answers not fitting across a narrow screen.

- **The engines' marks are the app's own, and a fetched favicon is measured
  before it is trusted** (`ui/EngineIcons.kt`, new; `res/drawable-nodpi/engine_*.png`;
  `SiteIcon`'s `glyphGround` / ink measurement, `Favicons.measureInk` /
  `IconInk` / `hasInk`, `FaviconStore.forget`). Bing's icon was missing from
  the strip, and chasing it turned up three separate things wrong with asking
  a favicon service for chrome.
  **What s2 serves for bing.com is a 32px WHITE magnifier on a transparent
  ground.** Not a placeholder — Bing's own icon, in the variant meant for a
  dark toolbar. On the light bar it was invisible, which is what "missing"
  was; on a white chip under it, invisible again. The device settled it:
  `files/favicons-128/bing.com.png` measured 3% coverage with every opaque
  pixel pure white.
  **So the five built-ins ship with the app.** Everything else here is a site
  the user happened to visit, where a missing icon costs a lettered square;
  these are chrome — the strip IS them, and one blank in that row is a control
  that cannot be read. Each engine's own mark (DuckDuckGo's 256px app icon,
  Google's branding G, the Fluent `b` off Wikimedia Commons, Brave's touch
  icon, Startpage's 192px tile), handed to `SiteIcon` as an already-captured
  bitmap so the tile, the corner and the lettered fallback stay one
  implementation. A CUSTOM engine keeps the fetch — a pasted URL's host is the
  only icon there is.
  **They are trimmed to their own ink and scaled to one optical size**, which
  is the square root of alpha MASS rather than a bounding box: a circle and a
  letterform of the same box do not read as the same size, and Google's G
  arrived edge-to-edge where Brave and DuckDuckGo carried their own margins —
  which is why the G looked twice everything else. Capped at 104 of the 128
  box so the tall `b` cannot tower. No grounds: Bing's white square is gone
  (the Fluent mark has real transparency, where the apple-touch icon is
  white-backed), and `glyphGround = false` stops `SiteIcon` putting back
  exactly the ground the asset had stripped out.
  **A fetched icon is now measured once per bitmap** (`measureInk`, a coarse
  grid) and two answers come off the one scan. Nothing drawn in it at all is
  not an icon: it falls back to the letter, and — this is the part that
  breaks the loop — a blank file is DELETED from the cache, since a cache with
  nothing in it answers every later fetch and no refetch is ever made. And a
  mark on a transparent ground gets a chip whose colour is chosen by the ink's
  own luminance: light ink on a dark chip, dark ink on a light one. A fixed
  white chip is what turned the white magnifier from invisible into invisible.

- **The omnibox veil's fade is drawn as bands, because the gradient never
  landed** (`engineStripVeil`, `VEIL_SLICES`, `EngineStrip.fadePx`,
  `ENGINE_STRIP_VEIL_FADE_STEPS`). The text under the strip was cutting back
  in on a straight line rather than fading, and the first two explanations
  were both true and both insufficient.
  **A Brush's shader is laid out in the CANVAS's coordinates**, from 0 to the
  size it is applied at — so a gradient stamped at `topLeft = edge` has run
  out before it starts and every pixel takes its clamped end colour, i.e.
  fully opaque. That is the straight line. Moving the canvas under it instead
  (`translate`) reads correctly and, measured on device against a screenshot
  of the same bar with no strip up, changed NOTHING: mask alpha went 0 to 1 in
  six pixels either way. Bands of flat colour are the one shape of this that
  provably lands — 24 of them across a fade this short is finer than the
  display resolves, each a plain rect inside a layer that is only asked for
  while a strip is up.
  **The ramp is smoothstep, not linear.** A straight ramp is continuous in
  value but not in slope, so both of its ends are a corner and the eye reads
  them as two cuts with a wash between them.
  **It starts at the last mark's INK and runs a third of a step.** It was
  measured off the icons' 40dp slots, where the marks are drawn at 30dp, so
  the first opaque pixel already sat past the last mark and the text arrived a
  whole fade beyond that — the gap. The length is a third of the strip's own
  pitch (published as `fadePx`, so it follows the step down when a narrow
  screen tightens the strip) rather than a number picked beside it: what the
  eye measures the fade against is the row it sits at the end of.
  **How it was checked**, since every part of this is invisible to reading:
  `adb exec-out screencap` of the bar with the strip up and with it down, and
  a per-column ratio of text ink between the two — which is the mask's alpha
  profile directly, and the only thing that told the working ramp from the one
  that compiled and did nothing.

- **A committed drop no longer slides the rows it displaced** (`SettingsSheet`'s
  `EngineArrangement`). Dragging an engine above the "Not offered" line made
  it appear to slide up from a row below where it belonged. The rows a drag
  displaces carry a full row of `translationY`; the drop then lays them out
  one slot along FOR REAL, and an `animateFloatAsState` retained across that
  reorder animates the stale offset away from a position that is already
  correct — the row jumping a slot and sliding back. `settle` is an
  `Animatable` keyed on the arrangement now, so a commit starts from rest
  while a drag in flight (which does not change the arrangement) still
  animates.

- **The engine picker is a row of the engines' own icons, and the swap is one
  icon travelling** (`NewTabSheet`'s `EngineButton` rewritten, `EngineStrip` /
  `engineStripVeil` / `EngineStripLatch.order` / `EngineStripPositionProvider`
  new or reworked; `Favicons.ICON_PX`, `FaviconStore`'s versioned directory,
  `SiteIcon`'s `filterQuality`). Tapping the search glyph used to drop a panel
  of five labelled rows over the history and suggestions the user was choosing
  between. It now unrolls a strip of the engines' favicons along the bar.
  **The strip's first icon IS the button.** The engine in use is drawn at the
  button's own place and the rest start stacked underneath it at exactly the
  size the button draws them, fanning out to the right and shrinking back into
  that same spot when one is picked — so choosing is not two marks
  cross-fading in a menu, it is one mark travelling to where the old one was,
  landing on it at its size as the button's own icon takes over (`alpha = if
  (progress > 0f) 0f else 1f`, a hard cut, because what replaces it is the
  same picture and a cross-fade of a picture with itself is a flicker).
  An earlier arrangement put the row to the RIGHT of the button and faded the
  button out under the pile: that left the omnibox with a hole where its
  search mark had been for as long as the strip was up.
  **Being level with the bar, it no longer cares how far the sheet is dragged
  up** — the drop-UP was refused outright at full sheet height, since its top
  half would have been off the screen, and `NewTabSheet` has lost the
  `sheetExpanded` parameter that existed for that.
  **Nothing is drawn under the icons** — no pill, no hairline, no lit ring
  behind the one in use. What makes room for them is the field's own text
  fading out from under them (`engineStripVeil`: the `fadeEdges` DstIn trick
  with a MOVING edge, so the gradient is one cached shader stamped at a
  per-frame offset rather than a `Brush` compiled per frame, and the offscreen
  layer is only asked for while a strip is up, or every keystroke into the
  omnibox would be composited offscreen). A ring around one icon says twice
  what its position already says; a panel would have been a surface inside a
  surface.
  **One animated float, read only in draw.** `progress` lives on the shared
  `EngineStrip` (the field has to yield to it as well) and every frame of it
  is a `graphicsLayer`/draw read — position, scale, per-icon stagger, the
  veil's edge — so a whole open costs no recomposition of the sheet. The
  hovered icon swells (`animateFloatAsState` read inside the layer) instead of
  lighting up, which is the one feedback that survives being under a finger.
  **The row's ORDER is settled in the event that opens it**, not in
  composition and not in an effect: it is "the engine in use, then the rest",
  and a selection changes which engine that is — so recomputing it in
  composition reshuffles the row half way through the collapse the selection
  started, and an effect writes it a frame after the frame that first draws
  the strip. `setOpen` is the one door in, and the gesture reads the order off
  the latch rather than closing over it, since a changed `pointerInput` key
  would cancel the very press that is opening the strip.
  **Icons are fetched at 128px** (`Favicons.ICON_PX`) and drawn with
  `FilterQuality.High`: s2 serves whichever of its own sizes is nearest, so
  asking for 64 and drawing at 30dp on a 3x screen was asking for a blurred
  upscale. The cache directory carries the size (`favicons-128`) and older
  ones are deleted on the way past, or the sharper request would be served out
  of the blurrier answer for the life of the install.

- **The omnibox picks its engine per query, and the engine list is the
  user's** (`core/SearchEngine.kt` rewritten as a data class; `BrowserStore`'s
  `disabledSearchEngines` / `customSearchEngines`, `BrowserViewModel`'s
  `allSearchEngines` / `enabledSearchEngines` / `setSearchEngineEnabled` /
  `addCustomSearchEngine` / `removeCustomSearchEngine`; `NewTabSheet`'s
  `EngineButton`, `SettingsSheet`'s `SearchEnginePane` + `AddEngineRow`).
  The + sheet's search glyph is the engine's own favicon now, and it is a
  button: it says "this is a search field" and WHICH search in one mark.
  **The choice it makes is the query's, not the app's.** `engine` is
  `remember(openKey)`-keyed exactly like the field beside it, so it is seeded
  from Settings on every open and thrown away with the sheet — picking Google
  for one lookup leaves the setting alone — and re-seeded by a
  `LaunchedEffect` if the setting changes under an open sheet.
  **The menu of labels this described is gone** — see the strip entry at the
  top of this list, which replaced it. What survives from it unchanged: the
  placement being a `PopupPositionProvider` rather than an offset on the
  popup's content (a popup's content is laid out in a window whose origin is
  NOT this one's — measured 125px out, the status bar — while `anchorBounds`
  and the returned offset are in one space by contract), and the popup not
  being focusable, or it takes the window's focus and drops the keyboard out
  from under the field.
  **Tap toggles; HOLD opens and selects by drag**, one detector because they
  are one press — the hold is the tap that hasn't ended yet. Two bugs found on
  device doing it: `waitForUpOrCancellation` gives up the moment the finger
  leaves the node, and this button is 32dp, so a press drifting three pixels
  on its way to a hold was neither; and movement inside the hold window has to
  be CONSUMED or the sheet's own drag claims the press half way, settling the
  sheet and dropping the keyboard.
  **A `pointerInput` block is a trap twice over, and both halves bit.**
  First, state read inside one is read through a snapshot that does not
  advance with the composition. Logged on device: the same
  `MutableState` object answered `false` to the gesture on every tap while the
  composition read `true` and drew the menu — so the second tap opened it
  again instead of closing it. `EngineMenuLatch` is a plain (non-snapshot)
  object written from the composition, and the gesture reads that: what was on
  screen as of the last frame, which is the only thing a finger can be
  answering anyway. The drag's hit-test reads the menu's placed top off the
  same latch, written by the position provider, so the CLAMP for a long list
  on a short screen can't put the rows somewhere the finger isn't looking.
  Second — and this is the one that made the menu unopenable for the rest of
  the process — the block also keeps everything it CLOSED OVER until one of
  its keys changes, callbacks included. `onOpenChange` went on writing to the
  `remember(openKey)` state of the sheet's PREVIOUS open, so after the + sheet
  had been closed and reopened once, every tap set a flag nothing was reading.
  The latch carries the live callbacks too, refreshed by the same `SideEffect`.
  The same rule is why the Settings drag works out its own `from`/`to` inside
  the gesture rather than reading them off the composition.
  **Settings arranges the list by hand instead of toggling it.** The pane is
  one column — the engines on offer, a line, and the ones that aren't — where
  a row's SECTION is its place in that column: dragging by the handle reorders
  the picker and, across the line, stops offering an engine, which is one
  gesture doing what an order and a switch used to do separately. Swiping a
  row away deletes a custom engine and, for a built-in, does the strongest
  thing there is (there is nothing to delete it FROM): moves it below the
  line, with `confirmValueChange` REFUSING the dismissal so the row snaps back
  and what the eye follows is it travelling to the other section rather than a
  gap. Rows are keyed by engine id, or a swiped-away row hands its position to
  whichever engine slides into that index — which showed as an empty red row
  in place of an ordinary one. Every row is 56dp INCLUDING the header, so a
  drag's travel divides into whole rows and the header is just a slot to be
  dropped past; `to` is a `derivedStateOf` so the other rows lay out a handful
  of times per drag while the float behind it changes every frame, and the
  held row reads that float in its `graphicsLayer` rather than in composition.
  A drop that would leave nothing on offer is refused in the UI, so the row
  snaps back instead of appearing to move and then not having. Tapping a row
  below the line puts it back on offer — the drag is the precise way, that is
  the quick one.
  **A tap anywhere else closes the menu and does nothing else.** That is a
  second popup window laid over the whole of the app's, UNDER the menu (popup
  windows stack in composition order), consuming the press on the way down and
  closing on the way up. It is a WINDOW rather than a scrim inside the sheet
  because the point is that the touch never reaches what it landed on: a
  non-focusable popup is told about outside touches, but so is the app window,
  so dismissing that way still opened the history row the finger came down on.
  Gated on `open` rather than on the transition, so the 140ms the menu takes
  to leave isn't 140ms of swallowed taps; the keyboard is unaffected, the IME
  not being this window's to cover.
  **The arrangement is written through immediately**, not on the 500ms save
  debounce: it is rare, deliberate and several drags long, and a session that
  ends before the debounce fires loses the lot while every earlier change
  survives — which reads as the list forgetting at random. (Seen once on
  device: a saved blob holding the final ORDER beside a disabled set from
  three edits earlier. Cause not established; the immediate write removes the
  race either way.)
  **Adding an engine is a dialog**, not a section that unfolds in the list: it
  takes the keyboard, and this window is `adjustNothing`, so the keyboard
  covers the bottom of a pane that has nothing below the fold to scroll out
  from under it. A dialog brings its own window and lands above the keyboard
  on its own. A newly added engine is named explicitly at the END of the
  order, since `orderEngines`' rule for engines the order doesn't mention
  exists for a built-in a later build adds and would file the user's own one
  among its neighbours.
  **`SearchEngine` is a data class**, not an enum: a custom engine has to be
  as much of an engine as DuckDuckGo is. Built-ins keep their old enum names
  as ids, so a save written before this reads back unchanged; `%s` (or
  OpenSearch's `{searchTerms}`) marks the query, and a URL with no placeholder
  gets one appended rather than being refused, since a pasted search URL
  ending in `?q=` is the commonest input there is.
  **Disabled engines are stored as the EXCLUSION**, so a build that adds a
  built-in offers it to everyone instead of hiding it from every save written
  before it existed. Switching off the default hands the default on rather
  than refusing the switch; the last enabled engine can't be switched off at
  all. Settings' list carries both questions — the row picks the default, its
  place decides whether it is offered — and the order it is dragged into is
  the order the picker offers.

- **A preview is the page, and it is taken while the page is still there**
  (`BrowserScreen`'s `pageExposed`, `BottomToolbar.onCoverPress`,
  `MainActivity.onPause`, `BrowserViewModel.capturePageNow` /
  `drawThumbnail`'s `isFlat` guard / `applyExactCapture`'s orphan check).
  Two complaints, and they turn out to have one shape: a copy is only worth
  anything if it is taken at a moment when what is on screen is the page and
  nothing else, and those moments were being both mis-identified and missed.
  **`pageExposed` was a second, hand-written list of the same states
  `pageIsWhatsOnScreen` already carries**, and two lists drift: Downloads and
  the long-press context menu were in one and not the other, so a preview
  could come back with the browser in it. It is now built ON TOP of that test,
  plus the surfaces that test deliberately does not count as covering the page
  because they sit beside it — the find bar and both password cards — each of
  which is still the browser's own pixels inside the window. The
  text-selection toolbar is left out on purpose: it is a floating window of
  the platform's, outside this one's surface, so a copy never sees it.
  **And it was published a frame late.** `LaunchedEffect` is dispatched
  through AndroidUiDispatcher, i.e. at the start of the NEXT frame, so the
  frame that first drew a sheet over the page was still telling the ViewModel
  the page was exposed — which is exactly the frame a copy asked for on that
  press lands against. `SideEffect` runs at the end of the composition that
  changed the state, before anything is drawn.
  **The press is the deadline, not the tap.** The tabs button already asked
  for its copy on pointer DOWN; the + and the menu button raise a sheet on the
  RELEASE and asked for nothing, so the page they cover was photographed only
  by whatever idle capture happened to have run. `onCoverPress` gives them the
  same treatment — a second detector consuming nothing, so the ripple and the
  click are untouched.
  **`onStop` is too late to photograph anything.** By then the window has no
  surface to copy out of, so `captureAndFlushThumbnails` is all software
  re-draws — and `View.draw()` reproduces nothing of a canvas and little of a
  composited page, so leaving the browser turned the card of the page you were
  reading into an empty rectangle. `capturePageNow` at `onPause` takes the
  true copy while the page is still up and still uncovered; the copy lands
  during the transition out, in time for `onStop`'s flush to write it.
  **A blank draw no longer overwrites a real preview.** That was the other
  half of the same bug and it stands alone: the software path does not merely
  fail to improve the card, it THROWS AWAY the copy taken while the page was
  on screen, with no way back. `drawThumbnail` samples a coarse grid
  (`isFlat`) and refuses when the result is one flat colour and the tab
  already has something. A genuinely blank page loses nothing by keeping the
  last picture of itself, which is why the test needn't tell the two apart.
  **And a copy for a tab that has since been closed is dropped rather than
  filed**, or `rememberThumbnail` queues a file under an id the next restore
  has no row to prune against.

- **A page that cannot scroll has its app shell shortened**
  (`PageBottomBar`'s `docScrolls` / `shellsFrom` / `applyShellCaps`, `capped`).
  claude.ai's sidebar ended under the toolbar — the account row, the Design
  row, everything below the chat list — and no amount of scrolling brought it
  back. That is a third case the two answers this file already had cannot
  reach.
  **The toolbar only ever uncovers its strip by hiding, and it only hides on
  a scroll.** So on a document that does not scroll at all, the bottom 81px
  are gone for good. claude.ai is a `100dvh` app shell: one full-viewport box
  inside another, each scrolling INTERNALLY, with the document itself fixed
  at exactly the viewport's height. Padding the body of such a page buys
  nothing — measured on device, our own padding was the only reason its
  document was scrollable at all, and dragging it did nothing a finger inside
  one of those internal scrollers could ever reach.
  **And nothing down there is `fixed`, so nothing down there is a bar.** The
  footer row is `position: relative` at the end of a flex column, three
  levels inside a box sized by its own `top` and `bottom`. Shifting a box
  that is IN FLOW is not an option: it would land on the row above it. So
  the viewport-sized boxes it lives in are capped by `max-height` to the
  height the user can actually see, and the shell lays itself out again
  inside them — which is the same thing as handing the page a shorter
  viewport, done the only way a page can be told from outside one.
  **Every viewport-sized ancestor, not just the outermost.** The sidebar is
  `absolute` with a `top` and a `bottom` of its own, so it takes no height
  from its parent: capping the parent alone left it hanging past the bottom
  edge, where it was then CLIPPED rather than merely covered — strictly worse
  than the bug. It is found on the second probe line (the inset's, which the
  bar detection already probes) when it opens after the caps are in place.
  **A capped shell is no longer viewport-tall, so no measurement can find it
  again** — the caps are RETAINED until the reason for them goes (the bar
  hides, the page grows something to scroll, the element leaves the
  document), and the element's own top is remembered rather than re-read, so
  a later change of inset re-lands its bottom edge exactly on the toolbar.
  **The body padding is still applied over the top of it, and costs nothing
  there**: the cap frees exactly the inset and the padding fills exactly
  that, so the document ends where it always did and still doesn't scroll.
  What it is there for is the shell that does not account for everything —
  content overflowing a `min-height: 100vh` wrapper, which the cap moves
  nothing of — where it is again the only way to reach the last strip.
  Gated on `!docScrolls()`, which discounts our own padding, so every
  ordinary page takes the exact path it did before (verified on device:
  m.youtube.com still scrolls, still gets its 57px of padding, and still
  rests its pivot bar on the toolbar's top edge, with nothing capped).

- **The blocker can be handed a file, and it stops being able to sit there
  doing nothing** (`BlocklistStore.SCHEMA` / `heal` / `ImportedList` /
  `importList` / `deleteImportedList`, `Config.importedLists`;
  `BrowserViewModel.importBlocklistFile` / `removeImportedBlocklist` /
  `blocklistImportError`, `loadBlocklist`'s second trigger; `AdBlockScreen`'s
  `ImportFileRow` / `ImportedListRow` and the blocked-on-page line).
  **The silent-failure state was a timestamp without a list.** Auto-update
  fired on AGE alone, so a `meta.json` written by a build whose feed ids no
  longer exist (`adguard` / `adguard_tracking` / `certpl` against the current
  `ads` / `trackers` / `cookies`) read as freshly updated while `hosts.txt`
  sat at 0 bytes — nothing matched, nothing was ever refetched, and the
  blocker looked implemented and did nothing. Three things now stand between
  that and the user: `meta.json` and `config.json` carry a `schema` stamp and
  a report from another version is discarded WHOLE rather than displayed
  (which is honest — those rows describe feeds that are gone — and makes the
  age gate fire), `heal()` deletes a zero-length `hosts.txt` and any leftover
  `.tmp` on the way in, and the ABSENCE of a downloaded list is its own
  trigger alongside age. An update from an ephemeral (overlay) session is
  refused: it reads the same directory the browser proper owns, and two
  sessions merging over each other is what this directory has no writer
  discipline for. It still blocks — it just doesn't maintain the list.
  **An imported file is copied, not remembered.** What the picker returns is a
  `content://` URI whose read grant belongs to this process and this launch,
  so a list kept as a URI works today and is gone tomorrow with nothing to say
  why. `importList` reads it once, writes the PARSED form (one domain per
  line, the shape `hosts.txt` already has) under `blocklist/imported/<id>.txt`
  and keeps only that id in the config; a row whose file has since gone is
  dropped at load rather than shown as a list with nothing behind it.
  **It is folded in at `buildIndex` time, like the user's typed rules and
  unlike a feed**, so an import blocks its first request on the next page load
  instead of waiting for a download — and it survives a failed update, since
  it was never part of the merge.
  **The MIME filter is `*/*` on purpose.** A blocklist arrives as
  `text/plain` from Files, `application/octet-stream` from most download
  providers and untyped from a few, so a text-only filter greys out exactly
  the hosts file the user opened the picker for. The CONTENT decides instead:
  a file too large to be a blocklist or with no parseable rule in it is
  refused, and the reason is put on the screen, because a picker that hands
  back a URI and nothing else makes unreadable, too big and empty look
  identical. `OpenDocument`, not `GetContent` — the system's file browser
  rather than whatever claims `GET_CONTENT`.
  Note KDoc and `arrayOf("*/*")` do not mix: Kotlin block comments NEST, so a
  ``text/*`` in a doc comment opens one and the next `*/` in real code closes
  the doc instead — the file parsed as garbage from that line down.
  **And `blockedOnPage` was being counted and shown to nobody** — it now has a
  line in the status block, which is the only place in the app that says the
  blocker did anything at all.

- **A page can open a second window** (`BrowserViewModel`'s `onCreateWindow` /
  `onCloseWindow`, `openWindowTab`, `create`'s `load` flag, `trimViews`'
  opener protection, `cookieManagerFor`; `CustomTabScreen`'s `tab`). Every
  WebView here was built with `setSupportMultipleWindows` at its default, and
  the `WebChromeClient` had no `onCreateWindow` — and the thing to understand
  is that WebView's default there is neither silence nor refusal. Chromium
  turns `window.open()` into a navigation of the view that CALLED it, and
  sets that window's `opener` to **itself**.
  **Which is how a working sign-in comes out looking broken.** The symptom
  was claude.ai's "Continue with Google": tapping it appeared to do nothing
  and loaded forever. Traced over the WebView devtools socket, the flow was
  in perfect health — `Page.windowOpen` for
  `accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth` with `userGesture=true`, the account
  chooser, the consent screen, all of it running in the tab it had just
  replaced. It ended on `accounts.google.com/gsi/transform`, whose entire job
  is to post the credential to `window.opener` and call `window.close()`.
  Evaluated in that page: `window.opener === window` -> **true**. It posted
  the token to itself; `window.close()` does nothing to a main frame; and
  claude.ai, navigated away in that same view, never heard anything. Blank
  page, forever.
  **So the window is a real tab**, with its WebView built on the spot rather
  than lazily by `webViewFor` — `onCreateWindow` answers the transport on
  that call and a live WebView is the only thing it can answer with. It
  arrives with NO url and `load = false`: the renderer navigates it itself
  the moment the transport takes it, `onPageStarted` fills the address in,
  and a load of ours would be a race with the page the popup was opened for.
  **An opener cannot be evicted while its window is open.** `trimViews` parks
  the page and DESTROYS the view, and that view is what the child's
  `window.opener` refers to — evicting it recreates the original bug by
  another route. The set is narrow by construction: only a window a page
  asked for carries an `openerTabId` at all.
  Gestures only, and `javaScriptCanOpenWindowsAutomatically` stays off, so a
  pop-under is refused twice over. `onCloseWindow` finds its tab by view
  IDENTITY — the callback carries the CHILD's view and the client belongs to
  the parent — and `closeTab`'s existing opener fallback is what lands the
  flow back on the page that started it.
  **The overlay follows the current tab, not the first**
  (`CustomTabScreen`). They are the same thing for as long as it is the one
  page it is meant to be; a popup is the one time they come apart, and
  showing the first tab through it would leave the flow running where nobody
  can see it.
  **Third-party cookies are now on**, which is a browser/embedder difference
  rather than a preference: off is right for an app embedding a page and
  wrong for a browser, where an OAuth popup, a checkout iframe and a sign-in
  widget are all a third party asking for the session the user already has
  with it. Set through `cookieManagerFor`, since a private tab's jar is its
  own profile's and not `CookieManager.getInstance()`'s.
  Separately noted while investigating, NOT fixed: Google One Tap logs
  `onetap.bottomSheet.prompt.unsupportedBrowser` on every load and always
  will — `IdentityCredential` is undefined, WebView has no FedCM, and
  spoofing the UA to plain Chrome does not change it. That is the bottom
  sheet, not the button. And `files/blocklist/config.json` on this device
  holds feed ids from an older build (`adguard` / `adguard_tracking` /
  `certpl`) against the current `ads` / `trackers` / `cookies`, so no feed
  matches, `hosts.txt` is 0 bytes and the ad blocker is silently doing
  nothing.

- **Editing an address is a change of SHEET, not a field that grows inside the
  menu** (`BrowserScreen`'s `addressEdit` / `cancelAddressEdit`, `MenuSheet`'s
  `onEditAddress`, `NewTabSheet`'s `editingUrl` / `pristineAddress`). Tapping
  the menu's address bar now swaps `sheet` to `Sheet.NewTab` with the current
  URL in its field, pre-selected. The two sheets were already one omnibox in
  two states sharing one surface, and everything a half-typed address wants —
  matching history, live search suggestions, the private-space button — was
  only ever built on the + sheet. The in-place `BasicTextField` had none of
  it and was a second implementation of the same field.
  **The prefilled URL is not a query.** `pristineAddress` (the field still
  equal to `editingUrl`) makes the list stay the ordinary recent /
  most-visited one until a keystroke changes it, so a sheet reached from the
  menu is as good for jumping somewhere else as the + sheet is. Matching
  against the address already on screen would answer with the page the user
  is standing on and hide everywhere they might go instead, and suggestions
  for a full URL are worse still. It is a comparison against the whole field
  rather than a flag, so deleting back to the original address is a return to
  the list rather than a search for it.
  **`Go` navigates the tab whose address it is**, not a new tab — read before
  `closeSheet`, which clears the target. That distinction is the only thing
  `editingUrl` buys `onGo`.
  **The private button stays hidden while an address is in the field**, on
  the existing rule that the clear button takes that slot whenever there is
  text. Offering to reopen the page the user is already on in a space it
  isn't in reads as a button with nothing to do with what is in front of
  them. It comes back the moment the field is cleared, and toggling it there
  still ABANDONS any edit (`openNewTabSheet()` with no target, which empties
  the field in the same composition), since the tab that address belongs to
  isn't in the space just switched to.
  **Back returns to the menu rather than closing both**, which is the step
  the tap took — same position in the chain the in-place edit held, and the
  same for the keyboard-dismissed-by-back path. One `LaunchedEffect(sheet)`
  clears `addressEdit` on every way OUT of the + sheet (dismiss, drag-down,
  `settleSheet`'s collapse, the hand-back to the menu) rather than each of
  them doing it; it cannot pull the keyboard back up, because the + sheet
  reads `visible` before re-filling its field and `visible` is already false
  through the close animation.
  `NewTabSheet.query` is a `TextFieldValue` now, since an address has to
  arrive SELECTED; `MenuSheet` lost its field, its `searchEngine` and its
  `onNavigate` with the branch.
  **The field is reset by a `remember` KEY, not by an effect** (`openKey` /
  `newTabSheetOpens`, and `openNewTabSheet` as the one door into the sheet).
  An effect runs after the frame that opened the sheet, and this composable
  never leaves composition — it sits at zero height between opens — so frame
  one was drawn with whatever the last open left in the field: a previous
  address, matched against history, flashed as a one-row "Matching history"
  list before the real list arrived. Keying the state on a counter the CALLER
  bumps rebuilds the field during the opening composition instead, which is
  why every `sheet = Sheet.NewTab` now goes through one function. Nothing
  clears `addressEdit` on the way OUT, either: the sheet is still drawn
  through its exit animation, so clearing it there would flip the list back
  to matching-history for the length of the slide. The next open sets it.
  **The domain row also gets a fresh `MutableInteractionSource` per open.**
  The tap unplaces this row in the same frame it fires, and the row stays
  COMPOSED — so nothing ever arrives to end that press, and the ripple it
  armed was still waiting to play the next time the menu was drawn: the
  long-press-on-a-link animation, seconds late, over an address nobody was
  touching. `remember(open)` hands the indication a source with no
  interactions in it, so there is nothing left to replay.
  **And the domain row only takes an offscreen layer when it needs one.**
  `fadeEdges` composites offscreen, and this row is unplaced and re-placed
  every time the sheet hands over to the + sheet and back (`placedWhen`) — a
  layer torn down and reallocated around the address, which showed as a dark
  frame over it on the way back in. It is now applied only when the host
  actually overflows the bar, the same test NewTabSheet's rows use.

- **The long press and the new tab are now the user's call**
  (`core/NewTabPlacement.kt`, new; `BrowserStore.Settings.linkPreviewEnabled` /
  `newTabPlacement`, `BrowserViewModel.toggleLinkPreview` /
  `setNewTabPlacement`, `newTab`'s `background`, `SettingsSheet`'s Behavior >
  Links, `LinkPreviewOverlay.onOpenInBackgroundTab`). Two settings over what
  a long press on a link does and where the tab it opens goes.
  **Quick preview is one branch in `openContextMenu`, not two paths.** The
  same press, the same `requestFocusNodeHref`, and then either a
  `LinkPreview` or the `WebContextTarget` the menu was always built from —
  which is why the floating Open / Open in new tab / Copy link menu needed
  nothing added to it: those rows never went anywhere, they just stopped
  being reachable for links. Toggling closes whichever surface is up, since
  it was raised under the rule that just changed. Images are untouched by the
  switch: `IMAGE_TYPE` takes the menu either way, there being nothing to
  preview.
  **A background tab is a tab nothing moves for.** `newTab(background = true)`
  still puts it at the end of the row — it is the newest tab whether or not
  it is being looked at — but skips `setCurrentTab` and `rememberPrevious`,
  so the double-tap flip does not come back to a tab the user has never seen.
  `openNewTabFromSheet` returns before its entrance animation for the same
  reason: nothing arrived on screen to animate.
  **The preview card cannot be PROMOTED into a background tab**, which is the
  one place the two settings meet. A promotion is the card growing into the
  page underneath and dissolving onto it — and that page is the one the user
  is staying on, so the expand would land on the wrong document. With the
  setting on Background the card's "New tab" opens the tab and dismisses
  normally instead (`onOpenInBackgroundTab`, which replaces the promoting
  handler rather than sitting beside it).
  **And it toasts**, which nothing else here does for a navigation: there is
  no tab count anywhere in the chrome, so a background open is otherwise
  completely invisible — the one case where saying so is the only feedback
  there is.

- **The switcher's ground is the empty screen's, and the face is under the
  last card as it leaves** (`EmptyState.kt`'s `Watermark`/`SwitcherBackdrop`,
  `BrowserPalette.switcherBg` DELETED, `PrivateSwitcherBackdrop` folded in).
  Closing the last tab is the switcher emptying out, not a move to another
  screen — so the two must not be two screens. `switcherBg` was
  `surfaceContainerHigh` against the empty screen's `surfaceContainerLow`, and
  the kaomoji only existed on the empty screen, so the last card's close was a
  change of shade with a cat arriving into it. `SwitcherBg` is now an alias
  getter for `emptyBg` rather than a second token that happens to match, and
  the watermark is drawn as the switcher's FIRST child in both switchers, so
  the departing card uncovers it.
  **What raises it is the COMMIT, not the tab count** (`closeCommitted` in
  `TabSwitcher`, `closingLastRow` in `TabListSwitcher`): while there is any
  chance the tab stays, the empty screen's face has no business being shown to
  a user who is not about to get one. So it is down for a row of cards, down
  for the last card sitting there, and down through every gesture on that last
  card — a swipe that may yet snap back, a tabs-button drag that ends with the
  page on screen again — and it goes up on the flick past the close threshold,
  on the pile's own release (which empties the switcher without ever being at
  one tab), or on the list row's close press, for the length of the exit
  animation. Instant, no fade: it appears while the card is still over it, and
  the reveal is that card leaving. Nothing clears the flag — the close lands,
  the tab list empties, and `BrowserScreen` swaps the whole switcher for
  `EmptyState`, taking the `remember` with it.
  **Which cat is a cross-fade on `LocalPrivacy`, not a read of the mode**,
  which is what lets one composable serve both spaces: the ordinary face at
  `1 - p` and the private one at `p`, so the two trade places over the length
  of the violet's own turn instead of being cut over a frame ahead of it. The
  `privateMode` parameter is gone from `TabSwitcher`/`TabListSwitcher` with
  it — the backdrop was the only thing reading it.

- **The menu's Dark tile is a per-tab override, not a second settings
  control** (`BrowserViewModel`'s `pageDarkOverrides` / `pageDarkFor` /
  `builtDark` / `resolvedPageDark`; `togglePageDark`, `syncPageDark`,
  `rebuildForPageDark(ids)`; `applyDarkening(web, active)`). The tile used to
  call `setPageDarkMode(On/Off)` **and** `setThemeMode(Dark/Light)`, so one
  tap rewrote both of Settings' own choices, persisted them, and left System
  behind on both — a page darkened for a minute silently became the app's
  answer for every launch after it. It is now what it looks like: this tab's
  page, made the other way, for as long as the user is on it. Like Desktop
  site and zoom, it is done TO the page in front of you; Settings is where
  "how should pages look" is answered, and the tile no longer answers it.
  **Per tab, because the WebView is where the state actually lives.**
  `prefers-color-scheme` is baked into the context a WebView was constructed
  with, so darkening a tab is a rebuild of that tab's view — there was never
  anything global about it except the flag being read. `pageDarkActive` is now
  the CURRENT tab's resolved value (override, else the setting), updated in
  `setCurrentTab`, which is why switching tabs re-reads it without rebuilding
  anything: that tab's view was already built with its own answer.
  **Toggling back to what the setting says drops the override rather than
  pinning the same value**, so the tab rejoins the setting instead of quietly
  ignoring the next change to it. Nothing is persisted — a relaunch is
  whatever Settings says, which is the whole point.
  **A settings change spends every override**, and `resolvedPageDark` is what
  makes that honest: `syncPageDark` is reached both by the page dark setting
  and by the chrome's dark state, and the second changes constantly without
  changing what pages resolve to (chrome going dark while pages are pinned
  Off). Clearing overrides on those would throw a tab's tile away for a
  decision that was not about pages. Only the views that DISAGREE with the new
  value are rebuilt (`builtDark`, a `WeakHashMap` of what each live view was
  actually built with) — a tab already dark under an override the setting has
  caught up with has nothing to redo.
  `pendingChromeDark` / `applyPendingChromeDark` and the `flushChrome`
  unwinding are gone with the chrome flip they existed to defer.

- **Dark mode stops theming sites that are already dark** (`PageDarkening`'s
  `alreadyDark`). YouTube came out inverted on top of its own dark theme, and
  the stand-down check that was supposed to prevent exactly that could never
  fire, for three reasons that each stand alone.
  **The check measured through our own stylesheet.** The filter's CSS forces
  `html { background-color: #fff !important }` — the canvas colour has to be
  something for the invert to land on — so reading the root's background back
  while that rule is live reads OUR white and concludes the site is light. The
  detection was self-reinforcing: once applied, it could never un-apply, which
  is why the periodic re-check (which was there all along) never caught a site
  that goes dark a moment after load. Every measurement now runs with the
  style element `disabled`, synchronously inside one task, so no frame is
  painted un-darkened.
  **And it looked at `body`, which on an app shell paints nothing.**
  m.youtube.com's background belongs to `ytm-app` several levels in, so the
  body-then-root walk found a transparent box on both and fell through to
  "light". Six points across the viewport are hit-tested instead and the first
  ancestor of each with an OPAQUE background votes — a majority, because one
  light card or a fixed banner over a dark page should not decide it.
  **The site's own theme flags are read first**, before anything is measured:
  `html[dark]` is exactly what YouTube sets, `data-theme` / `data-color-mode` /
  `data-bs-theme` and a `dark`/`night` class are what most of the rest set, and
  these are true before first paint — which is what keeps a themed site from
  flashing inverted for a frame while the first measurement waits for layout.
  A `light` flag is not a veto, it just isn't a dark one; the painted vote still
  decides.
  Standing down is now `disabled` rather than removing the `<style>`, since a
  site can turn its own dark theme back off, and a `MutationObserver` on the
  attributes of `<html>` and `<body>` re-runs the check on the frame a theme
  flips rather than up to one 400ms tick later.
  Verified off-device by running the real `ENABLE` script against a stub DOM
  (`node`): plain light page, light app shell, `html[dark]` + dark shell, dark
  shell with no flag, `color-scheme: dark` vs `light dark`, `data-theme=dark`,
  `theme-light`, plus the regression itself — filter applied to a light page,
  site turns dark, filter stands down, site returns to light, filter re-arms.

- **Leaving fullscreen no longer leaves the page shortened by the toolbar**
  (`PageBottomBar`'s `appInset`/`inset` split and `applyInset`). The bottom
  inset is now zero for as long as the document is in fullscreen, and the
  shifts are given back on the way in rather than on the way out. Nothing of
  ours is over that window — Chromium's own view is what is on screen, and the
  toolbar this whole file accounts for is not drawn on it — so a page in
  fullscreen was being told its bottom edge was covered by 81px that were not
  there.
  **The page's fullscreen element is exactly the shape the probe looks for**:
  `fixed`, full width, resting on the bottom edge, and too tall to be a bar —
  i.e. a PANEL, which is shortened by its own `bottom`. That write outlives
  the fullscreen it was made in: YouTube's `.player-container` comes back to
  its ordinary `top: 48px` layout still carrying `bottom: 81px !important`
  from us, stretches to fill the gap between the two, and the watch page is a
  black full-height player from then on. **And it cannot recover on its own**,
  which is the part worth remembering — the shift is what keeps the box
  resting on the inset's line, and resting on that line is what keeps it
  recognised as a panel, so the retention loop re-applies its own cause. Only
  a reload cleared it.
  `fullscreenchange` drives it both ways (plus the `webkit` alias), and the
  handler is the same one `__bottomBarInset` now calls, so there is one path
  that changes the inset and one place that unwinds the shifts.

- **The five things a page can ask for that were answered with silence**
  (`ui/WebPlatform.kt` and `core/FileDownloads.kt`, both new; `BrowserViewModel`'s
  "web platform requests" section, `BrowserScreen`, `CustomTabScreen`,
  `ImageSaver`). `WebChromeClient` here overrode three methods — title, icon,
  progress — and the thing to understand about the rest of it is that the
  DEFAULT for every one is silence rather than refusal: an unhandled file
  input does nothing when tapped, an unhandled fullscreen request leaves the
  video in its box, an unhandled `confirm()` resolves to false without the
  user ever being asked, and no `setDownloadListener` means a download link
  is a tap that appears not to register. The app looked like it had decided
  against these features; it had never been asked.
  **All five are held as state on the ViewModel and drawn by one composable**,
  because they share the one property that matters: each is a request already
  in flight inside the renderer that needs exactly one answer. `WebPlatform`
  is therefore a single line at the END of both `BrowserScreen` and
  `CustomTabScreen` — last so its `BackHandler`s outrank the chain above them
  (a later registration wins) and so it draws over everything, and one line so
  the link overlay gets the same five without growing its own half of each.
  Each request carries a tab id: the page that asked can stop being the page
  on screen while the question is up.
  **A dropped callback is not the same as a declined one, and this is where
  the bugs live.** A `ValueCallback` from `onShowFileChooser` that is never
  called leaves that form's input dead for the rest of the page's life, so a
  cancelled picker still reports (`parseResult`'s null answer), and a chooser
  that is replaced is answered before it is replaced. A `JsResult` that is
  never answered stops that document's script thread for good, so a
  navigation cancels the outgoing document's dialog rather than dropping it,
  and a second dialog raised while one is up is declined outright — a page can
  call `alert()` in a loop, and queuing those is handing the browser over to
  it. The fullscreen View is BORROWED from Chromium and handed back by
  `onRelease`; it is not assumed to arrive parentless, since a video handing
  off to another can bring the same view back while it is still in a
  container. `exitFullscreen` clears the state BEFORE calling
  `onCustomViewHidden`, which comes straight back as `onHideCustomView`.
  **The download carries the page's session by hand.** `DownloadManager` runs
  in another process and inherits nothing, so the cookies, `Referer` and
  user-agent WebView would have sent are attached explicitly, or a file behind
  a login arrives as somebody's sign-in page saved under a `.pdf` name. The
  cookies are read where the WebView is, in `create`, because a private tab's
  session lives in its own profile's jar (`WebViewCompat.getProfile(web).cookieManager`)
  and the default one holds a different user or none. `data:` is decoded and
  written here — DownloadManager cannot take it at all — and `blob:` is a
  plain explained failure, since it exists only inside the renderer.
  `FileDownloads.saveBytes` is the write both this and `ImageSaver` now go
  through, so there is one answer to "where did that go".
  **A certificate warning is remembered per host, and that is what makes it a
  question rather than a loop.** The default `onReceivedSslError` is
  `cancel()` with nothing said — safe and illegible. But a page with twenty
  subresources on one broken certificate raises twenty of these, so asking
  twenty times is asking nobody: the answer is kept per host for the life of
  the process (never written to disk — a relaunch asks again) and a second
  error arriving while the card is up is refused rather than queued. Proceed
  is drawn in the error colour and does NOT get the accent: the safe answer is
  the one that should look like the answer. The `beforeunload` message the
  page supplies is deliberately not rendered — every browser stopped years
  ago, because it is attacker-controlled text in a dialog the user cannot
  leave.
  **`ModalCard` is a card, not a sheet**: every sheet in this app is a place
  to go next, and these are the surfaces that STOP something. Tapping the
  scrim does nothing on purpose — something in the renderer is blocked until a
  button is pressed, and a dismissal that can happen by accident is not an
  answer. The body scrolls inside the card rather than growing it off the
  screen with the buttons, since a page chooses that text and its length.
  Fullscreen also hides the system bars and holds the screen awake: a video is
  the one thing the browser shows that is watched without being touched, which
  is exactly when the display times out.
  `pageExposed` now counts all three of these as over the page, or a preview
  would be a copy of a dialog.

- **Zoom is a stepper in the menu, and the number is the reset**
  (`BrowserViewModel.zoomIn`/`zoomOut`/`resetZoom`, `MenuSheet`'s `ZoomRow`,
  `BrowserStore.Settings.pageZoom`). Minus and plus either side of the current
  percentage, directly under the tiles — zoom is another thing done TO the
  page in front of you, like Dark and Desktop, not a place to go. It isn't a
  tile itself because a tile has one state and this has eleven.
  **The readout in the middle is the button.** Tapping "175%" puts it back to
  100, which is why it is accented whenever it is off 100 and takes no press
  and no ripple at 100: the one place the current value is shown is the one
  place it can be undone, so a page left zoomed can't be a mystery the next
  time the menu is opened.
  **It is `textZoom`, not a page zoom, and there is no third option.**
  WebView has no page-zoom API — `setInitialScale` sets a starting scale and
  is undone by the first pinch or by any page declaring its own viewport —
  while `textZoom` is honoured by responsive layouts, which reflow to it
  instead of growing a scrollbar sideways. The ladder is the one every desktop
  browser uses (50…200), whose gaps widen as they go so that one press is a
  similar-sized change at either end. One setting for every live view rather
  than per site or per tab: a browser this size has nowhere to hang per-site
  settings, and a background tab left at the old scale would change size under
  the user when they switched to it.

- **Clear browsing data** (`BrowserViewModel.clearBrowsingData`,
  `SettingsPane.ClearData`). `clearHistory` emptied two in-memory lists and
  nothing else — no cookies, no cache, no `WebStorage` — so the one thing a
  user reaches for when they want to be signed out of everything did not do
  it. Four switches and one button, rather than a row per kind that clears on
  the spot: they are almost always wanted together, and four separate
  destructive rows are four chances to hit the wrong one. They are separate at
  all because they answer different questions — history is what you did,
  cookies are who the sites think you are, the cache is only what was faster
  to keep, site data is what pages stored themselves — and signing out of
  everything versus unsticking a broken page should not be the same press.
  Form data goes with the cache: it is the same kind of leftover and has no
  switch worth adding.
  **What is deliberately not swept up**: the open tabs and their back stacks
  (clearing what has been visited should not close what is being read — and a
  page on screen is not information the browser is holding, it is the thing
  the user is looking at), and the saved passwords, which have their own list,
  their own delete-everything row and a device lock in front of both. Deleting
  those from behind that door without ever opening it is not what this button
  says.

- **Two arrangements for passwords, never both at once** (`BrowserViewModel`'s
  `externalPasswordManager` / `ownPasswordsActive` / `applyAutofillImportance`;
  `ui/AutofillProviders.kt`, new; `PasswordsScreen`, `PasswordPrompts`,
  `BrowserStore.Settings`; `core/GooglePasswords.kt` and both
  `androidx.credentials` deps DELETED). Settings > Passwords is a **Save
  passwords** switch and a *Managed by* choice between two apps: **Yuku**, or
  the password manager the user has actually chosen on this device.
  **Not a switch labelled "Google", because that would be describing the
  mechanism wrongly.** What the flag reaches is the platform autofill service
  — whichever app fills that role, Google Password Manager or Bitwarden or
  1Password — so the row reads the chosen one's name off
  `Settings.Secure.autofill_service` and names it. There is no public API for
  that question; `AutofillManager.isEnabled` is the public one and answers a
  smaller version of it (is anything answering at all), which is the fallback
  when the read comes back empty. Naming the package needs a `<queries>` entry
  for `android.service.autofill.AutofillService` or it is invisible on API
  30+. The choice itself lives in system settings — an app cannot appoint
  another app's autofill service — so picking the external side with nobody in
  the role opens the picker instead of selecting a manager that isn't there.
  `ACTION_REQUEST_SET_AUTOFILL_SERVICE` is launched with NO data URI: a
  `package:` URI means "ask the user to make THIS app the provider", which is
  not what a browser wants.
  **External: the browser is out of it entirely.** Fields go to the service
  (`IMPORTANT_FOR_AUTOFILL_AUTO`), which both saves and fills, per site, from
  the keyboard's strip. Everything here stands down — nothing captured,
  nothing prompted, no fill bar, and no vault row in Settings, since a list of
  logins from before the switch is furniture with nothing behind it. The vault
  is HIDDEN, not emptied.
  **Yuku: the browser is the password manager and says so to the system** —
  the fields are withheld (`IMPORTANT_FOR_AUTOFILL_NO_EXCLUDE_DESCENDANTS`) so
  the keyboard cannot offer a login beside our own bar offering the same one.
  That is why the flag is driven from this choice rather than being a setting
  of its own: the two surfaces are exclusive by construction, not by luck.
  **Credential Manager is gone, and that is a fix rather than a cut.**
  `CreatePasswordRequest` files a login under the calling APP, not the site
  (per-site origin needs `CREDENTIAL_MANAGER_SET_ORIGIN` plus Google's browser
  allowlist), so a password "saved to Google" that way reached the account but
  came back matched to nothing — autofill matches on `webDomain`, which our
  entries were never filed under. Letting the manager's own autofill do the
  saving files it under the site correctly. The site-in-the-id encoding, the
  picker chip, `SavedPassword.google` and ~160 lines of API went with it.
  **What this cannot be narrower than**: autofill has no per-field-type switch
  reachable from an app, so choosing Yuku withholds saved addresses and cards
  from the keyboard too, not only logins. The caption says so, and it is why
  the default is the external manager.
  Deliberately NOT done: making Yuku an `AutofillService` so its own vault
  reaches the keyboard strip. `Settings.Secure.autofill_service` holds exactly
  one component, so that would replace the user's manager for every app on the
  device — a browser has no business making that claim on their banking app's
  behalf, and they would have to select it in system settings regardless.

- **Saved logins come from the keyboard now** (`BrowserViewModel.webHostContext`
  + `attachHost`/`detachHost`, `MainActivity`, `CustomTabActivity`;
  `GooglePasswords`, `PasswordStore`, `PasswordsScreen`, `PasswordPrompts`,
  `BrowserStore.Settings`). The platform autofill service was never reaching
  this app's pages, and the reason was one line: **every WebView was built on
  the Application context.** WebView reports its form fields to autofill
  through the ordinary View callbacks, with the page's domain attached
  (`ViewNode.getWebDomain()`) — which is what makes a provider fill per SITE
  rather than per app — but the session doing it is opened against the
  Activity the view belongs to (`Context.getAutofillClient()`), and Chromium
  decides whether to build an autofill provider for the WebView at all by
  unwrapping its context looking for an Activity. With no Activity in that
  chain there is no client, no provider, and no suggestion: the feature looks
  unimplemented when in fact it was never asked for.
  **The context is a `MutableContextWrapper` whose base is swapped**, an
  Activity while one is hosting the tabs and the Application when none is —
  because the other half of the original reasoning was right too. These
  WebViews are held in a ViewModel across configuration changes, so an
  Activity context held directly is a whole Activity leaked per tab. The swap
  has to be in place BEFORE a WebView is constructed (Chromium reads the
  context once, at construction), so `attachHost` is called from `onCreate`
  ahead of `setContent`, not from anything composed; `detachHost` is
  identity-checked in `onDestroy` so a replaced Activity can't take its
  replacement with it. The overlay does the same for its own session.
  Inline suggestions — the strip inside the keyboard — need API 30+ plus an
  IME that supports them (Gboard does); below that the same suggestions come
  as a dropdown over the field. Either way it is the system drawing it.
  **Sign in automatically is gone.** Filling a form the moment it appears was
  a decision with one right answer only where exactly one login was saved,
  and it now competes with a system-drawn suggestion that is scoped to the
  site properly.
  **Google Password Manager is one switch: save there too, or don't.** The
  read-back half went with it (`GooglePasswords.pick`, `PickOutcome`,
  `decodeId`, `SavedPassword.google`, the suggestion bar's Google chip).
  Credential Manager cannot be asked "what do you have for this site" — no
  query without UI, and no per-site scope without a privileged path an app
  can't grant itself — so that chip could offer everything EXCEPT the entry
  for the page in front of the user. Autofill answers the same question
  properly. `save` and `encodeId` stay: a login kept here really does reach
  the user's Google account. The browser's own suggestion bar now appears
  only when the vault has something for the site.
  **Settings > Passwords is four rows and a locked door.** Save passwords /
  Saved passwords / Save to Google / Keyboard suggestions, with the vault
  moved one step further in (`SavedPasswordsPane`, `SettingsPane.SavedPasswords`)
  behind the device's own fingerprint-or-PIN (`ui/DeviceLock.kt`, new;
  androidx.biometric, which is what forces `MainActivity` to be a
  `FragmentActivity` — `BiometricPrompt` takes nothing else). The prompt is
  asked for on the way IN, not raised by the pane once it is up: a list that
  appears and then covers itself has already been on screen. A device with no
  lock screen falls through rather than being refused its own passwords. That
  lock is a door on the SCREEN, not on the file — `PasswordStore`'s key is not
  `setUserAuthenticationRequired`, since that would mean re-authenticating for
  the synchronous read in its `init` that has to happen before the first page
  can be filled.
  `SettingsPane` gained a `parent`, because back had been "return to Root"
  everywhere and there is now a pane two steps in; its declaration ORDER also
  matters, the pane transition taking its direction from the ordinals.
  **Keyboard suggestions is a real switch, not a link to system settings**:
  off sets every WebView's `importantForAutofill` to
  `NO_EXCLUDE_DESCENDANTS`, and since a WebView's fields are a *virtual*
  structure it provides on request, declining to be asked is declining the
  feature outright — no session, nothing in the strip. Applied to every live
  view on toggle, not just the current tab. Note what this pane no longer
  has: the door to `ACTION_REQUEST_SET_AUTOFILL_SERVICE`. The switch governs
  whether this app offers its fields; which provider answers is still only
  choosable in system settings.

- **Two switches for who owns a link** (`core/ExternalLinks.kt`, new;
  `BrowserStore.Settings`, `BrowserViewModel`, `CustomTabActivity`,
  `SettingsSheet`'s Behavior > Links). Both default ON, i.e. the behaviour
  that was already there plus the handoff Chrome does.
  **"Links from other apps as overlay"** decides what a tapped link in
  Telegram gets while this is the default browser: the overlay
  (`CustomTabActivity`) or an ordinary new tab. Off, `onCreate` hands the URL
  to `MainActivity` and finishes *before* `setContent` — read off the
  ephemeral session, whose settings restore synchronously in its `init`, so
  the overlay's window never has content in it. That handoff also gets
  `overridePendingTransition(0, 0)`: the theme's fade over the caller is a
  transition for a window nobody is meant to see. The menu's "Open in Yuku"
  keeps its animation (`animated` defaults true).
  **"Open links in apps"** is `shouldOverrideUrlLoading`, which this app
  simply did not implement before — so a `steam://` or `intent://` link used
  to land on WebView's own "webpage not available". `ExternalLinks.route`
  returns one of three things (`Browser`, `Consumed`, `LoadInstead`), and the
  third is why it isn't a boolean: an `intent://` URL's `browser_fallback_url`
  is a DIFFERENT url from the one the WebView was handed, honoured both when
  the switch is off and when nothing is installed.
  **A scheme the browser cannot render is not the user's decision**
  (`ALWAYS_EXTERNAL`: mailto, tel, sms, geo, market). Refusing those doesn't
  keep the link here, it drops it — so they leave whatever the switch says,
  and a page's own app scheme with nothing installed is swallowed rather than
  loaded, since an error page over what the user was reading says less than
  staying put.
  **The https half is deliberately narrow**: main frame plus `hasGesture()`
  only. A scripted redirect or an iframe hopping into another app is a
  hijack, not a choice. "Is there an app for this" is `queryIntentActivities`
  minus the general browsers, and those are found by probing a host under the
  reserved `.invalid` TLD — no app link can be verified against it, so
  whatever answers handles the web at large. That set is cached for the
  process; the per-link query isn't. The launch carries `setPackage`, or an
  implicit VIEW just raises a chooser with every browser on the device in it.
  An `intent://` string is composed by the PAGE, so its selector, component
  and flags are stripped before launch and only a browsable VIEW survives.
  The manifest's existing `<queries>` VIEW/BROWSABLE/https element is what
  makes any of the resolution visible on API 30+.

- **Links from other apps open as an overlay** (`CustomTabActivity`,
  `CustomTabsProvider`, `core/CustomTabRequest.kt`, `ui/CustomTabScreen.kt`, all
  new; `BrowserViewModel`'s `ephemeral` mode, manifest, `Theme.Browser.CustomTab`
  + `res/anim/custom_tab_*`). A tapped link in another app used to open the whole
  browser, in the browser's own task, on top of the user's session. It now opens
  one page with a header — close / address bar / overflow — and closing it lands
  back in the app the link came from.
  **The address bar is the browser's own omnibox at header scale**
  (`AddressPill`): same `FieldBg` pill, same centred lock-and-domain, and the
  same behaviour when tapped — a field carrying the full URL, pre-SELECTED so
  one keystroke replaces it, Go navigating this overlay's page through
  `UrlUtils.toUrlOrSearch`. An overlay is one page, but the page it lands on
  isn't necessarily the last one the user wants, and a bar that only reports is
  a dead end. While editing, the flanking close and menu buttons give up their
  space to the field (the trade the menu sheet's row makes) and back cancels the
  edit before it means anything else — and so does a tap on the PAGE, which
  gets an invisible scrim for the duration: without it the tap reaches the
  WebView, which takes the focus and drops the keyboard while leaving the bar
  sitting in a half-finished edit. No shadow on it, unlike the browser's
  omnibox: that one floats over a page on a sheet, this one is set into a solid
  bar, where a drop shadow only muddies the pill's edge. The header is 64dp
  rather than the 56dp the browser's own bars are — a 44dp pill in a 56dp row
  has 6dp of air, which reads as crammed against the page once the eye is
  comparing the whole strip against the status bar above it.
  **Both of the overlay's own surfaces animate, in the app's vocabulary.** The
  menu is a `Popup` rather than a `DropdownMenu` because Material's own
  transition runs on a clock that can't be reached from outside it (120ms in,
  75ms out): it now grows out of the button it hangs from and shrinks back into
  it (`TransformOrigin(1f, 0f)`, [Overshoot] in at 180ms, [Accelerate] out at
  140ms), with a `MutableTransitionState` keeping the popup window alive for the
  length of its own exit — dropped on the boolean alone, the window is torn down
  on the first frame and there is nothing left to animate.
  **It has a hairline border and NO elevation shadow, and that is a fix rather
  than a style choice.** The platform draws an elevation shadow from the layer's
  OUTLINE and refuses to draw one at all while that layer's alpha is below 1 —
  so on a surface that fades, the shadow snaps off on the first frame of the
  exit and snaps back on only at the end of the entrance. Measured off a slowed
  capture (`settings put global animator_duration_scale 10`, then screencaps
  through the exit): the page just outside the panel's edge goes from a 220→237
  gradient to a flat 238 in ONE frame, before the panel has visibly moved at
  all. What that looks like is not "the shadow is late" — it reads as the CORNER
  changing shape, since a rounded corner with a halo looks rounder than the same
  corner without one. The radius never changes; the same capture puts the arc
  profile at an identical 9/5/2/1/0 inset in every frame. A border is drawn as
  content, so it fades and scales with the panel and there is nothing left to
  pop. The enter fade is also shorter than the scale it runs alongside (100ms
  against 180ms), so nothing is still arriving by alpha as the menu settles. The address bar turns
  over 200ms: the flanking close and menu buttons shrink their WIDTH rather than
  fading (`expandHorizontally`/`shrinkHorizontally`), so the field growing and
  the buttons leaving are one movement — the field grows into the space they
  vacate — while the pill's two faces cross through a blank middle on one
  animated float, the outgoing fading over the first half and the incoming over
  the second.
  **That turn is deliberately not a `Crossfade`**, which is the obvious way to
  write it and is a trap: `Crossfade` puts its content in a Box of its own, so
  the field's `weight(1f)` becomes RowScope parent data the enclosing Row never
  sees. It compiles — the receiver is still in lexical scope — and silently lays
  the field out at its own text width with the clear button on top of it. One
  face at a time, inside the Row, is what keeps the layout honest. The focus
  request moved with it: the field asks for focus when it ENTERS composition,
  half a turn after `editing` goes true, since a `FocusRequester` that isn't
  attached to anything yet has nothing to give focus to.
  `fadeEdges` goes on a WEIGHTED row with
  the lock and domain centred inside it, never on the Text: on a wrap-content
  Text — exactly as wide as its own letters — it eats the first and last
  character of every domain instead of the space beside it, which is what its
  own doc warns about.
  **The overflow menu is Material 3's menu spec, stated as constants**
  (`MENU_ITEM_HEIGHT` 48dp, `MENU_GUTTER` 12dp, `ICON_SIZE` 24dp,
  `MENU_DIVIDER_GAP` 8dp) because the top row of icon buttons — reload, share,
  copy link — has to obey them too: one row tall and as wide, so they carry the
  same touch target as the rows. Their SPACING is the one thing not taken from
  the rows — spread evenly across the full width (`SpaceEvenly`, equal gaps
  between them and at both ends) rather than aligned to the rows' gutter, which
  pushes the outer two into the corners and reads as three separate things
  instead of one row, with hairline `VerticalDivider`s an icon tall between
  them. Their sizing is optical rather than geometric: Material's glyphs all
  live in a 24dp box but do not fill it equally — the refresh arrow is inset
  noticeably further than share and copy, so it gets its difference back
  (`ICON_SIZE_REFRESH`, 27dp) to be the same size to the EYE, which is what
  "the same size" means for a row of glyphs. Below the divider: Open in Yuku,
  Bookmark, Desktop site, Find on page — the top row's three are deliberately
  NOT repeated there. One icon family throughout (Outlined), with the app's
  filled/outlined convention kept for state: a bookmarked page and desktop mode
  fill their glyph in. The container is 260dp wide with a 20dp corner, both
  passed to `DropdownMenu` itself (`shape`, `containerColor`) rather than to a
  background modifier, which would paint a square inside the rounded surface.
  **A bookmark is the one thing the ephemeral session keeps.** It can't go
  through `persistNow` — that would write the overlay's empty tab list over the
  real session — so `toggleBookmark` does a read-modify-write of that one list
  via `BrowserStore.updateBookmarks`, and `MainActivity.onStart` calls
  `reloadBookmarks` so the browser proper picks up what an overlay added while
  it was away instead of saving its own older list back over it.
  **The whole mechanism is task placement, not a window trick.** `CustomTabActivity`
  has `android:taskAffinity=""` and nothing asks for a new task, so a
  `startActivity` from Telegram stacks it on TELEGRAM's back stack; `finish()`
  pops it and the chat is there underneath. That is also what Chrome's custom
  tab is — there is no API for floating over another app, and the ones that
  look like it (`SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW`, bubbles, PiP) are either policy-hostile
  or can't host a page. The VIEW/BROWSABLE filter MOVED here from
  `MainActivity`, which keeps LAUNCHER + APP_BROWSER (the pair that puts the
  app in Settings > Default apps — the role is satisfied per PACKAGE, so the
  two filters living on different activities is fine) and still takes the
  explicit VIEW intent "Open in Yuku" sends it.
  **`CustomTabsProvider` is why an app picks us at all.** An app calling
  `CustomTabsIntent` first asks the package manager which browser exports
  `android.support.customtabs.action.CustomTabsService`; with no answer it
  falls back to its own WebView or to a bare VIEW intent. The service itself
  does little — `warmup` builds one throwaway WebView to pull Chromium's
  native library into the process while the user is still looking at the
  calling app, and `mayLaunchUrl` does the same and says so honestly, since
  there is no public WebView API to prerender or even preconnect a URL from
  outside a live page. An app with its own embedded browser never asks, and
  nothing here overrides that: a built-in browser is the app's decision.
  **The session is a second ViewModel that never writes**
  (`BrowserViewModel(ephemeral = true)`, via `EphemeralFactory`). Two
  Activities are alive at once and each gets its own ViewModel, so two
  `BrowserStore` writers would be two browsers saving over each other.
  Ephemeral restores the user's SETTINGS only (`restoreSettingsOnly` /
  `applySettings`, split out of `restoreSavedState`) and no-ops `markDirty`,
  `persistNow`, `savePageStates`, `rememberThumbnail` and `captureThumbnail` —
  a preview has no card to go on here, and a copy of the window is pure cost.
  `parkedFromDisk` is guarded too, and that one is a real bug avoided rather
  than tidiness: an ephemeral session numbers its tabs from 1 like any other,
  so id 1 would have read the REAL session's parked page and restored somebody
  else's tab into an overlay opened on a link.
  **What the overlay does NOT take from the caller is its colours.**
  `EXTRA_TOOLBAR_COLOR` is parsed by nobody: the chrome here follows the
  user's accent and their light/dark setting, and a Telegram-blue bar in a
  violet private session is a clash, not continuity. The caller's own MENU
  ITEMS are honoured (below a divider, fired with the current URL as the
  intent's data), since those are function rather than decoration — minus the
  ones that duplicate a row we already have (`OWN_ACTIONS`): every client is
  written against a browser whose menu it cannot see, so Telegram adding its
  own "Copy link" is the normal case, not a quirk. What survives the filter is
  the caller's genuinely own actions, "Open in Telegram" and the like. The
  navigation callbacks are honoured too
  (`CustomTabEvents` — clients hang their own spinners on those, and every
  call is best-effort because the callback is a Binder into a process that may
  be gone).
  **Back walks the page's history first**, then closes — after find and the
  long-press menu, the same order the browser's own chain uses. The page ends
  above the keyboard by way of `imeAnimationTarget` (the height it is GOING to
  be, so the page reflows once rather than per frame), exactly as the main
  screen does it, and there is no bottom toolbar to hide under: an overlay has
  no toolbar, so the page is simply the screen minus the header and the insets.
  **The window animation is a fade, not a page transition.** A full-height
  slide is how Android says "you have gone somewhere else"; this window is
  meant to say "this is on top of what you were doing", so it FADES in over
  240ms with a 6%-of-screen rise under it to give the fade a direction, and
  leaves on a 180ms fade with a 4% drop (`Accelerate` from `ui/Motion.kt` as a
  `pathInterpolator` resource, `res/interpolator/accelerate_leave.xml`). The
  caller gets an explicit no-op animation of matching length — without one the
  platform's default slides the app out from under us, which is exactly the
  reading being avoided, and it also has to still be DRAWN for every frame the
  overlay fades in over it. Shorter than the 300ms of the app's own
  full-screen surfaces: less ground to cover, and an overlay that takes as
  long to arrive as a page transition stops feeling like one. No overshoot on
  the way up: this settles at 0 in POSITION, and carrying past would lift the
  page off the bottom edge and show a strip of the app underneath — the same
  rule the in-app surfaces follow. `Theme.Browser.CustomTab` also drops
  the splash cats (`windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` `@null`, neutral ground):
  this window opens over somebody else's app, and the browser's launch
  ceremony there reads as being yanked out of it.
  Known limits, all deliberate for now: browsing in an overlay is not recorded
  in history (an ephemeral session has nowhere to put it that wouldn't fight
  the real one's save), and `EXTRA_INITIAL_ACTIVITY_HEIGHT_PX` — Android 12+
  partial custom tabs, the half-height sheet with the caller visible above —
  is not honoured, since window translucency has to be decided before the
  window is created and can't be switched from `onCreate`.

- **Swipe the toolbar's end buttons to walk the tab row** (`BottomToolbar`,
  `BrowserScreen`'s `beginSwipeSwitch`/`swipeSwitchDrag`/`swipeSwitchEnd`,
  `BrowserViewModel.stepToTab`/`neighbourTab`/`promoteSteppedTab`). Dragging
  RIGHT off the tabs button (the bar's left end) pulls the tab to the left in
  from the left edge; dragging LEFT off the menu button pulls the next one in
  from the right. It is the double-tap flip's own animation, driven by the
  finger instead of a clock: `QuickSwitchOverlay` took a fixed
  `QUICK_SWITCH_DIRECTION` and now takes a `direction` parameter, since this
  gesture genuinely goes both ways.
  **That animation is a slight zoom out and a slide**, not the recents drum it
  started as. The pair steps ~8% back from the glass, slides across with an
  8dp strip of background between them, and comes back to the glass as it lands — both pages carrying the SAME zoom on every frame, which
  is what makes it one strip being pushed along rather than two cards moving
  independently. The perspective tilt and per-card depth it replaced stretched
  the near edge at full-screen size and was a lot of motion to spend on one
  step sideways. The zoom is a clamped parabola (`pullBack`) peaking mid-slide
  and zero at both ends, so the animation starts and ends at exactly the size
  and place of the live page behind it — no seam at either handoff — and the
  clamp is what keeps the overshoot (which only the SLIDE is allowed to take)
  from asking for a page larger than the screen with a negative corner radius.
  300ms, now the turn is gone.
  **The two are placed by the distance between their CENTRES** (`separation`),
  not by a screen width plus an offset. A page scales about its own centre, so
  two of them a fixed distance apart pull their facing edges away from each
  other as they zoom out: an 8% zoom on a 1080px page opened ~86px of
  background between them, widest exactly mid-slide where it is most visible.
  Deriving the separation from the live scale (`width * scale + gap`) holds
  that strip at 8dp on every frame instead. Nothing commits until release —
  past `SWIPE_SWITCH_COMMIT` of the screen or a flick past
  `SWIPE_SWITCH_FLING`, or the card goes back off the edge it came from — and
  the landing (`landQuickSwitch`, factored out of `quickSwitchTabs`) still
  holds the incoming image over the WebView until it has actually painted.
  **The row must hold still or the second swipe means something else.**
  `selectTab` moves the tab it lands on to the END of the list, which is right
  for a switcher tap and wrong for walking a row by position: step left onto
  the neighbour and it is instantly the newest tab, so the tab just left is no
  longer on the right and the way back is gone. `stepToTab` therefore switches
  WITHOUT reordering. The move is deferred, not cancelled — the tab is
  remembered as owing one, and `promoteSteppedTab` pays it the moment anything
  wants most-recently-used order (`openSwitcher`, the tabs-button drag that
  opens it, `selectTab`, `newTab`, a space switch). So a run of swipes reads as
  a row being walked, and the tab it ends on is still the newest card when the
  switcher opens.
  **One node, two gestures.** The tabs button already carried the drag-up-into-
  the-switcher gesture, so its detector now axis-locks: the first NET
  displacement past a 12dp dead zone decides which gesture this is, once
  (`ToolbarDrag`). Net, not summed |dy| as before — a diagonal that ends up
  mostly sideways is a swipe however wanderingly it got there, where summing
  distance committed it to the switcher on the way past. A horizontal drag with
  no tab to step to (or the wrong way round for that end of the bar) becomes
  `Rejected` and is swallowed for the whole gesture, rather than left to be
  re-tested a few pixels later and turn into a switcher drag halfway through.
  The menu button keeps its `IconButton` (and its ripple) with the detector on
  its own modifier; it only consumes once the swipe is recognised, so the tap
  is untouched.

- **Measured against Arc Search: the app is not the problem, the debug build
  is.** On a 120Hz device, per `dumpsys gfxinfo framestats`. Both apps are
  driven at the same 8.33ms vsync, so the display is not it either.
  **Smoothness is cadence, not average frame time**, and that is the whole
  finding. Arc's frames are SLOWER than ours — 13.7ms each at launch, 18.3ms
  through its sheet, against our 11.3ms and 19.8ms — and it feels smoother
  because 118 of 118 of them present on consecutive vsyncs. A deep pipeline
  absorbs a long frame; it cannot absorb a variable one.
  **The release build presents on consecutive vsyncs too**: 114/115 through
  the tab switcher, 59/61 through the sheet, with recomposition at 0.61ms and
  draw recording at 0.37ms per frame — at parity with Arc's 0.63/0.49, and a
  faster cold launch than Arc's (862ms vs 977ms).
  **The debug build does not**: 107/115 and 39/47, with gaps of 2, 3, 4, 6 and
  11 vsyncs, twice the UI-thread cost, and a p99 of 40ms against release's
  12ms. Everything that reads as "not smooth enough" is in that gap. See the
  build section above for how to measure it properly.
  What the numbers say is still worth acting on, in this order: the UI thread
  is where our variance lives (the switcher's `anim+recompose` is 1.77ms
  against the sheet's 0.61ms even in release, and `FloatingTabLabel`
  deliberately recomposes per frame), and the GPU is the switcher's real
  ceiling at 12.6ms per frame — full-screen grain, a full-screen thumbnail and
  the live WebView all compositing at once.

- **Inertia, and the per-frame recompositions that were eating it**
  (`ui/Motion.kt`, new; `BrowserScreen`, `TabSwitcher`, `TabListSwitcher`,
  `BottomToolbar`, `SettingsSheet`, `ListScreens`, `WebContextMenu`). Two
  halves.
  **The vocabulary.** `Motion.kt` is now the one place that decides what
  arriving, settling and leaving feel like. Everything that ARRIVES somewhere
  carries a little past it and settles back — `Overshoot` (~1.2% past, peaking
  at ~60% of the duration), `OvershootSoft` (~0.5%, for motion crossing a whole
  screen where the same percentage is a much bigger movement). The percentage
  is of the TRAVEL, not of the element, which is what keeps it honest across
  sizes: a sheet moving 900px carries ~11px past, a menu scaling 0.9 -> 1
  lands at 1.001. These started four times larger and were cut deliberately —
  at 5% the settle is a wobble you can point at; at 1.2% it is not something
  the eye separates from the movement, it just stops reading as though the
  animation had been cut off at its mark. Everything that
  LEAVES takes `Accelerate` instead: an exit has nothing to settle onto, and
  overshooting one either happens off-screen or reads as hesitation. Durations
  given an overshoot are ~15% longer than they were, so the moment they FIRST
  reach the target is where they used to finish and the settle is what is new.
  Deliberately easing curves on ordinary tweens, not springs: nearly every
  animation here is a duration something else is timed against (a cover held
  for the length of a slide, a page inset that steps in one go while the bar
  takes `TOOLBAR_SLIDE_MS`), and a spring has no duration to quote.
  **Expanding and contracting a tab carries too** (`SHRINK_INERTIA`,
  `shrinkOf` in `BrowserScreen`). The page grows a hair past fullscreen and
  settles back onto it, and shrinks a hair past its card slot and settles back
  out to it. That needed the shrink's own mapping to change: `progress` is
  clamped to `[0, MIDPOINT_PROGRESS]` because past the top the gesture is
  translating an already-shrunk page around, so a settle that overshot simply
  saturated. `shrinkOf` is now that mapping with a narrow band past each end
  for the overshoot to land in — a BAND on the shared function rather than a
  second animated value, so the live page, its stand-in and the grid's row
  zoom cannot disagree about it. Three things fall out: the corner radius must
  NOT follow the band below zero (a negative radius is not a shape), the
  `progress == 0f` gates became `<= 0f` or the live WebView hides behind its
  own still image for the last frames of every expand, and dragging past
  "fully open" now spends its first ~1.2% of travel finishing the band instead
  of holding — about three pixels.
  **Where the overshoot deliberately isn't.** Any settle landing at 0 still
  overshoots in SIZE but never in POSITION: past 0 the page is off-centre at
  full size and uncovers a strip of bare background, whereas a page grown
  slightly larger than the screen is clipped by the window and uncovers
  nothing. And the quick-switch flip lets only the slide carry past square — fed to the tilt and scale it would be a card
  turning backwards, and a corner radius cannot be negative. The sheet
  pre-measures its contents to `OVERSHOOT_PEAK` rather than to the rest height,
  or every frame of the overshoot re-measures the thing the pre-warm frame
  exists to pay for once; the full-screen destinations extend their own `BarBg`
  past their bottom edge to fill the strip theirs uncovers.
  **The smoothness half is the same bug the sheet's height modifier already
  documents, on a bigger surface.** `progress`, `offsetX` and `offsetY` were
  read as `.value` in `BrowserScreen`'s own scope, so every frame of every
  switcher gesture recomposed the Scaffold, the toolbar, both sheets' full
  contents, every card in the grid, and `WebViewHost`'s `AndroidView` update
  lambda — which does real View-world work per run. They are now read in the
  layout/draw phase: composition gets three BOOLEANS off `derivedStateOf`
  (`progressAtZero`, `progressAtRestOpen`, `progressEngaged`), each changing a
  couple of times per gesture, and everything needing the number is handed a
  `() -> Float` it calls inside a `graphicsLayer`. Same treatment for
  `TabSwitcher`'s `zoomScale` and `stackProgress` (with `piled`/`pileFormed` as
  the coarse booleans for z-order and hit testing), `TabListSwitcher`'s fade,
  and `BottomToolbar`'s two icon rotations and the loading line. The one
  unavoidable composition read is `FloatingTabLabel`, whose width is a layout
  input `TabLabelRow` measures text against — it is read INSIDE that composable
  so the per-frame recomposition is confined to one row of text.
  **And the destinations no longer compose on the frame they start animating.**
  `AnimatedVisibility` composes its content on the first frame of the enter
  transition; these screens are lists of several dozen rows, i.e. tens of ms
  against an 8.3ms budget, so the first thing each did was hitch. `Destination`
  (in `BrowserScreen`) is that same slide-and-fade hand-rolled around the
  sheet's own fix: a pre-warm frame where the content is composed and laid out
  and the draw is skipped outright (`drawWithContent`, not alpha 0 — that still
  rasterises the whole screen on the frame that can least afford it).

- **The keyboard shortens the page; the bottom-bar shim stops jerking**
  (`BrowserScreen`'s `pageOverflow`/`imeUp`, `PageBottomBar`). Two things the
  shim could never do, both answered the way the platform says to: **the app
  is responsible for sizing the WebView.** Chromium does not resize a WebView's
  viewport for the on-screen keyboard (their Android OSK intent-to-ship says so
  outright — "no intended behavior change for Android WebView, and the Android
  app is responsible for sizing the WebView"), and the Android edge-to-edge
  guidance for WebViews is the same shape: put it in a container and apply
  systemBars + displayCutout + **ime** insets to that container.
  **So the WebView now ends at the top of the keyboard.** Until the view it is
  in actually gets shorter the renderer does not know the keyboard exists:
  `visualViewport` never changes and the field the user just tapped is never
  scrolled into view, because as far as the page is concerned it already is.
  `pageOverflow` is therefore allowed to go NEGATIVE — the WebView is smaller
  than the page's box while the keyboard is up, rather than hanging below it —
  and the JS inset goes to 0 for that time, since the page now ends above the
  keyboard and the toolbar is behind it. `setPageOverflow` still gets the
  clamped value: a preview is the page box, and there is no strip under the
  toolbar to leave out of it when the WebView is the smaller of the two.
  It reads `WindowInsets.imeAnimationTarget`, not `WindowInsets.ime`: the live
  inset climbs frame by frame as the keyboard slides, and following it is one
  reflow per frame. The target is the height it is going to be, known from the
  first frame, so the page reflows once — and once more, early, on the way out.
  **The jerk was the shim shifting a sheet mid-entrance.** Two causes.
  Panels were taken from every entry in the hit-test stack, so amazon.com's
  location sheet moved, and so did the scrim, and so did `a-page` — the whole
  page held fixed behind the modal, sliding around behind it. Panels now come
  only from the TOP of the stack, the thing the user is actually looking at.
  And the measurement was up to `THROTTLE_MS` late: amazon mutates
  continuously, so the throttle is always saturated and the sheet was first
  measured half way up its own slide (`@551-1213` in the logs) and shifted 81px
  right there. While the picture at the bottom edge keeps CHANGING the next
  measurement is now taken on the next frame instead (`settling`, capped at
  `SETTLING_FRAMES` so a permanently-animating page falls back to the
  throttle), which catches the sheet on the first frame it is eligible at all,
  with a few pixels showing, and lets its own animation carry it the rest of
  the way. Confirmed in the logs: a smooth monotonic 487→434 with the bottom
  landing on 782, the toolbar's top edge, and 45 measurements for the whole
  entrance.

- **A canvas app's preview stopped being its loading screen**
  (`BrowserViewModel.create`'s touch listener, `BottomToolbar` `onTabsPress`,
  `BrowserScreen`). kontur.systems is a WebGL map: it never scrolls the
  document, never reports progress again after load, and never navigates — so
  the LAST thing that ever queued a preview was `onPageFinished`'s
  `notePageMoved`, 250ms after a page whose tiles had not arrived yet. That
  picture then stayed on the card forever, because the two other chances both
  miss:
  **The switcher's own capture is asked for too late.** `captureAllThumbnails`
  ran from `openSwitcher`, i.e. on the tap's RELEASE, which starts the shrink
  in the same frame — and a `PixelCopy` lands a frame or two later and is
  dropped if the page is no longer the thing on screen by then (the
  `pageExposed` re-check at landing, which is there to keep the switcher's own
  flat background out of the card). It is now also asked for on pointer DOWN
  (`onTabsPress`, from `detectTapGestures`'s `onPress`, before the touch is
  known to be a tap at all): a finger takes tens of milliseconds to lift, which
  is all the frames the copy needs.
  **And the software fallback cannot draw a canvas** — `View.draw()` reproduces
  none of it — so there was nothing to fall back TO.
  The general fix is the third one: the finger lifting off the PAGE now calls
  `queueCapture`. It is the one signal that is always there for content that
  repaints with nothing an app outside the renderer can hear — a map panning, a
  canvas game, an SPA swapping a view. Deliberately `queueCapture` and not
  `notePageMoved`: the latter also licenses a software draw to REPLACE a good
  copy, and the software path is exactly what cannot reproduce the canvas that
  made this necessary.

- **The page is told where the toolbar is** (`core/PageBottomBar.kt`,
  `BrowserViewModel.setPageChromeInset`, `BrowserScreen`). The live WebView is
  laid out full-height with the toolbar drawn OVER its bottom strip (that's
  what keeps a hide from flashing the window background — see `pageOverflow`),
  so anything the page pins to the bottom edge sat *under* the bar until a
  scroll happened to hide it. `PageBottomBar` already found those bars for the
  system-navigation-bar floor; it now also pushes the covered strip back into
  the page and moves the page out of the way of it.
  **Bars are shifted by their own offsets** — `bottom` plus the inset, `top`
  (where it is also set) minus it, `margin-bottom` for anything positioned some
  other way. Each of the three was added because the one before it wasn't
  enough. Margin alone: a sticky box is held against the scrollport by its
  bottom inset and its margin doesn't enter into that at all, so
  auto.ria.com's `.main-sticky` sat exactly where it started with a 57px margin
  on it. `bottom` alone: kontur.systems' bottom sheet is
  `top: 129; bottom: 0; height: 734` pushed back down by a `translateY(650)` of
  its own, so its visible peek hangs off its TOP edge — moving only the bottom
  SHORTENS the box and moves nothing the user can see. Moving both slides it,
  keeping its height and whatever transform the site is animating it with. Not
  a `transform` of ours, which is how those bars animate themselves in and out
  — an inline one fights the site's own show/hide and can pin a hidden bar on
  screen.
  **A bar is measured by what it puts on screen**, not by how tall its box is
  (`min(bottom, vh) - max(top, 0)`): that same sheet is 734px tall against an
  839px viewport and would fail any cap meant to keep modals out. And once
  something IS being shifted the size test is dropped entirely, so a sheet
  dragged open past the cap doesn't fall back down under the finger opening it.
  **What is too big to be a bar is a panel**, and a panel is not ignored — it is
  the fallback when the probe finds no bar at all. amazon.com's location picker
  is `.a-sheet-web-container`, a fixed sheet most of the screen tall with its
  actions held against its own bottom edge; the toolbar covered them. A panel
  is SHORTENED by its bottom only, never slid up by both offsets (which would
  hang it off the top edge), so whatever it holds at its bottom comes up with
  it. It is not reported as a bar either: the reported height is what decides
  whether the page's inset stops at the system navigation bar, and a modal
  being up is no reason to hold that strip open for the page underneath. The site's own value is read once (before anything is written) and
  added to, `!important` so a site's own `!important` rule doesn't outrank the
  inline style, and given back when the element stops being a bar or the inset
  goes away. Writes are skipped when the value is already there — otherwise the
  script's own MutationObserver chases them and re-measures forever.
  **The document also gets `padding-bottom` on the body**, which is the other
  half of the complaint: the layout viewport already includes the strip the
  toolbar is drawn over, so a page's scroll runs out with its last content
  still under the bar and pulling back up to read it is what brings the bar
  over it again. The padding is exactly the inset and goes away with it.
  The probe reads the whole stack under each point (`elementsFromPoint`), not
  just the topmost element: a full-bleed overlay — a `pointer-events: none`
  toast layer, a portal root — is what `elementFromPoint` answers, and the bar
  underneath it is its sibling rather than its ancestor.
  `--browser-bottom-inset` is set on the root element too, for a page that
  would rather handle it itself.
  **A bar is recognised by where it rests, which is what makes the ordering
  matter.** With an inset applied the hit test probes two lines — the inset's
  and the viewport's own bottom edge — since a shifted bar rests on the first
  and one that just appeared on the second. And when the inset CHANGES, the
  bars already known are moved *before* the next measurement: one still
  carrying the old margin rests on neither line and would be measured as gone,
  which is the same report that takes the floor out from under it.
  The inset is the app's, not the document's, so a fresh page reads it back
  through the bridge (`Bridge.inset()`) at document start rather than waiting
  to be pushed — otherwise every navigation lands a page under the toolbar
  until the next change. It follows `toolbarVisible` rather than the slide's
  own value: the page shifts in one step while the bar takes
  `TOOLBAR_SLIDE_MS` to get there, so a bar coming up finds the space already
  made for it. Verified off-device by running the real script against a stub
  DOM (shift/restore round-trip, no repeated style writes at rest, no presence
  flapping across a hide/show, the padding staying stable), and on-device by
  logging the measurement out of `console` and reading it back through
  `adb logcat` (`chromium` tag carries console messages) — which is how the
  sticky case was caught: the bar was found and the margin was applied and the
  rect had not moved a pixel. auto.ria.com (sticky), m.youtube.com (fixed, top
  and bottom both set) and kontur.systems (the sheet) all come to rest with
  their bottom edge exactly on the toolbar's top edge, and amazon.com's
  location sheet was driven open and closed on-device (`adb shell input tap`
  against `screencap`) to check both the shift and that closing it puts every
  offset back.

- **A tab swap no longer lands on an empty page** (`BrowserViewModel`,
  `BrowserScreen`). Four separate causes, all of which ended as blank pixels
  where a page should be:
  **Eviction threw the page away.** `trimViews` called `destroy` directly, so
  an evicted tab lost its back stack and scroll offset and `create` fell
  through to a bare `loadUrl` — a cold network load under a cover measured in
  frames. It goes through `parkAndDrop` now (which already existed for
  `rebuildForPageDark`, and which `savePageStates` already assumed was being
  used: its "evicted by trimViews" branch was unreachable until now), so the
  tab comes back as the page it was on. `views` is also touched on every
  `webViewFor`, making its insertion order least-recently-USED rather than
  least-recently-created — a `getOrPut` never reorders, so the oldest-created
  view stayed the eviction candidate forever, and that is exactly the tab a
  double-tap flip returns to.
  **The cover was a frame count.** `HANDOFF_COVER_FRAMES` after `selectTab`.
  But a swap REPARENTS the WebView, so the incoming one has been detached
  since it left the screen and Chromium rasters it from nothing — a duration
  belonging to the page, not to a number of frames. Now
  `awaitPagePainted` (`postVisualStateCallback`, capped at
  `QUICK_SWITCH_PAINT_MAX_MS`), preceded by two frames for the reparent
  itself: it happens in `WebViewHost`'s update lambda, and asking a WebView
  that is not in the tree yet for its next frame asks nothing at all.
  **The cover could itself be blank** — `QuickSwitchFace` drew `PageBg` for a
  tab with no thumbnail, i.e. an empty screen for the whole flip. It draws the
  switcher's own `TabLabelRow` centered instead.
  **And a good preview could be overwritten with an empty one**, two ways.
  `PixelCopy` lands a frame or two after it is asked for, and the flip raises
  its overlay on the very next frame, so the copy could bake the overlay's flat
  background into the outgoing tab's card — `applyExactCapture` is now gated on
  `pageExposed` at LANDING, not only at request time. And `drawIntoBitmap`
  refuses a WebView that isn't attached: only one is in the view tree at a
  time, so every background tab's view is detached and its `draw()` came back
  blank — reached whenever that tab's content had moved on since its last copy
  (a background load finishing, an ad, a feed), which is how a perfectly good
  preview turned into an empty one on its own.

- **Double-tap flip turns one way, always** (`BrowserScreen.kt`,
  `quickSwitchTabs` + `QuickSwitchOverlay`). The deck's direction used to be
  derived per flip from the two tabs' positions in the row
  (`toIndex > fromIndex`). That looked equivalent to "the other tab is the one
  to the left" and isn't: `selectTab` moves the tab it lands on to the END of
  the row, so it only holds once a flip has happened — on a cold start the
  restored current tab needn't be last, so the session's FIRST flip turned the
  other way and every one after it turned back. Now a fixed
  `QUICK_SWITCH_DIRECTION = -1f`: incoming enters from the left, outgoing
  recedes to the right, every time. `fromRight` is gone from the overlay.

- **Find on page** (`ui/FindBar.kt`, new; `BrowserViewModel`, `MenuSheet`,
  `BrowserScreen`). The menu's "Search on page" row was a stub; it now opens a
  floating bar in the omnibox's own shape — same 56dp pill, same 24dp corner,
  same 6dp shadow and `FieldBg` — with the query field on the left and
  up/down/close on the right (36dp buttons rather than MenuSheet's 40dp, since
  three of them plus the "3/12" counter share the right end with a field that
  still has to be readable).
  **At the bottom, riding the keyboard**, anchored to `max(ime, toolbar)` in
  `BrowserScreen` exactly like the password cards — same `adjustNothing`
  caveat, so below API 30 the IME reports nothing and the bar stays where the
  toolbar left it. Gated on the same "nothing else is over the page" test
  those cards use, and wired into the `BackHandler` chain below the sheets and
  above the switcher. It takes that spot *from* the password prompts while
  it's up (`passwordsOverPage` now requires no find bar): they share one
  anchor, and find is the one the user is actively driving. The pill takes a no-op `detectTapGestures` — it floats over a live
  WebView, and a tap on the bar but not on a control would otherwise reach the
  page.
  **The state is per tab** (`BrowserViewModel.FindState`), because
  `findAllAsync` highlights inside one document: a count rendered over a
  different tab's page is counting matches nobody can see. So the bar closes
  on a tab switch (in `setCurrentTab`) and on the tab's next `onPageStarted` —
  after a navigation the highlight is gone and re-running the query would
  scroll the user somewhere they didn't ask to go. Counts come back on
  `WebView.setFindListener`, installed per WebView in `create` and guarded by
  tab id; WebView reports a **0-based** ordinal, so `activeMatch` is derived
  from the count (`if (matchCount == 0) 0 else ordinal + 1`) rather than
  trusted alone. Every keystroke re-runs `findAllAsync` — that's what makes
  the counter live — and an empty query is `clearMatches()`, not a search for
  `""`, which WebView answers with nothing at all and would leave the last
  query's highlight on the page. Enter is bound to **next match**, not to
  re-running the search, which would only throw the user back to the first
  one. The field keeps its own `TextFieldValue` locally (selection and
  composing region live there; pushing text back down per keystroke fights the
  IME for the cursor) while the query itself lives in the ViewModel.
  `openFind` refuses when the tab has no live WebView — the empty new-tab
  state has a tab but no document, and a find bar there takes the keyboard for
  a search that cannot match.

- **Saved passwords, and Google Password Manager alongside them**
  (`core/PasswordStore.kt`, `core/PasswordForms.kt`, `core/GooglePasswords.kt`,
  `ui/PasswordsScreen.kt`, `ui/PasswordPrompts.kt`, all new; `BrowserViewModel`,
  `BrowserStore`, `SettingsSheet`, `BrowserScreen`, manifest backup rules).
  Settings' "Saved passwords" row was a stub; it is now a pane with two
  switches (**Save passwords**, **Sign in automatically**), the vault itself,
  and the Google arrangement.
  **The vault is the one secret this app keeps**, so it is the one thing NOT in
  `BrowserStore`'s plain JSON blob: `filesDir/passwords/vault.bin`, AES-256-GCM
  under a key generated into the AndroidKeyStore. GCM rather than CBC because
  the file has to be tamper-evident as well as unreadable — an edited vault
  fails to authenticate and is discarded instead of decrypting to something
  plausible — and the IV is the cipher's own, read back per write, since
  reusing one under a GCM key is the mistake that actually breaks it. The key
  is not backed up, so neither is the file (`xml/backup_rules.xml` +
  `xml/data_extraction_rules.xml`, both new, wired into the manifest):
  otherwise a restore lands a vault on a device whose keystore never held its
  key. Entries are keyed by registrable domain + username, the same key every
  other list here already uses, which is looser than a real browser's
  origin-scoped store and is why nothing is ever filled unless the page has a
  password field on screen.
  **The page side is one document-start script and one bridge**
  (`PasswordForms`), because from outside WebView there is no other door.
  "The user just logged in" has no single event, so capture hangs off several:
  `submit` in the capture phase, a wrapped `HTMLFormElement.prototype.submit`
  (which fires no event at all), Enter in the password field, a click on
  whatever is playing the part of a button, `pagehide`, and `history.pushState`
  for the SPAs that sign in without navigating. Whichever fires first wins;
  the rest dedupe in the page.
  **A capture is not a prompt.** The page has only said the button was pressed,
  not that the site accepted it, so the credential is parked and raised on the
  tab's next `onPageStarted` — the navigation a real login causes — with a 2s
  timer behind it for the SPAs that never navigate. That is what keeps a
  mistyped password out of the save prompt.
  **Nothing on the bridge returns a password.** A page's own scripts can call
  every method on it (same window), so the worst a hostile page can do is ask
  for a save prompt whose contents the user can read and decline. Filling is
  Kotlin-initiated only, through `window.__pwFill`, and goes in via the native
  `value` setter plus `input`/`change` events — assigning `el.value` leaves a
  React-rendered form's own state untouched, so the field looks filled and the
  submission goes out empty.
  Auto sign-in fires on the *form-present* report rather than on page finish,
  only where exactly one login is saved for the site, once per page load, never
  in the private space, and **never submits** — filling a login and sending it
  are not the same decision.
  **Google's half is honest about its ceiling.** `CreatePasswordRequest` really
  does put the login in the user's Google account, but Credential Manager files
  it under *this app*, not under the site: per-site entries are a privileged
  path (`CREDENTIAL_MANAGER_SET_ORIGIN` + a Google-maintained browser
  allowlist) an app cannot grant itself. So the site is written into the
  credential's id (`"user · host"`, decoded from the right), and reading back
  cannot be silent — `getCredential` always shows the system sheet — which is
  why the suggestion bar offers Google as a *chip to tap* and why
  `SavedPassword.google` exists at all: it is the browser's own note that a
  site has something waiting over there, the only way to know. The pane also
  links to the system autofill settings, which is where Google's other answer
  for a browser (filling WebView's fields directly, per site) actually lives.
  Both prompts are cards above the toolbar rather than sheets — a login form is
  something the user is in the middle of, and a sheet would cover the field
  being filled — anchored to `max(ime, toolbar)` inset. Note the window is
  `adjustNothing`, so below API 30 the IME reports nothing and the card stays
  where the toolbar left it.

- **Private mode is a space, not a per-tab flag** (`ui/PrivateVisuals.kt`, new;
  `BrowserViewModel`, `BrowserStore`, `ui/theme/Theme.kt`, `MainActivity`,
  `BrowserScreen`, `NewTabSheet`, `MenuSheet`, `TabSwitcher`,
  `TabListSwitcher`, `SettingsSheet`). `Tab.isPrivate` still exists and still
  does what it did (private profile, nothing to disk), but it is no longer
  something you pick per tab: `privateMode` selects which of two tab spaces is
  open, and the two never appear together.
  **The split.** `_tabs` keeps holding both spaces — persistence, eviction,
  thumbnail capture and page-state parking all work across the lot — and the
  filtering happens in `BrowserScreen` (`allTabs.filter { it.isPrivate ==
  privateMode }`), synchronously in composition rather than as a derived flow
  in the ViewModel. That is deliberate: a `combine(...).stateIn` seeds with its
  initial value, and one frame of "no tabs" makes `LaunchedEffect(tabs.isEmpty())`
  throw up the new-tab sheet over a session that is actually there. Inside the
  ViewModel the same idea is `spaceTabs()`, which every "pick the next tab"
  decision now goes through (`closeTab`'s fallback, `closeAllTabs`, the switch
  itself) — falling back to a tab from the other space would put a page on
  screen that isn't in the list. `_currentTabId` is written only via
  `setCurrentTab`, which also keeps each space's own copy, so switching back
  lands where you left rather than on whichever tab is newest; the ordinary
  space's copy is what `persistNow` writes, since the private one is never in
  the saved tab list. `newTab` switches space if asked for a tab that doesn't
  belong to the open one (an external VIEW intent, which now just says
  `newTab(url)`), or the tab would be invisible until the user happened to
  switch over.
  **The visuals.** `BrowserTheme` now lerps between THREE ends, not two: the
  user's own light and dark schemes (the existing `darkness` animation) and a
  fixed dark `PrivateAccent` one, layered on top by a second animation
  (`privacy`, 420ms, published as `LocalPrivacy`). Private is always the DARK
  scheme whatever the app or the system is set to — a pale violet browser is
  not a signal anyone reads as "private" — and it's a lerp rather than a switch
  so going in and coming back out is the same kind of movement the light-dark
  turn is. Two things fall out of that: `LocalChromeDarkness` is now
  `t + (1 - t) * p`, so the system bars' icons count the private scheme as dark
  in step with the turn; and it's a *third* scheme rather than a re-seed of the
  other two, since the two animations are independent (the system can flip to
  dark halfway through going private).
  **Settings is the exception** — it re-provides the ordinary theme for its own
  subtree with a nested `BrowserTheme(privateMode = false)`, because it is
  where the ordinary theme is chosen and an accent swatch list rendered in
  violet shows the user a lie about what they're picking. `SystemBarIcons`
  takes an `ignorePrivacy` flag for the same reason (Settings covers the bars
  too), and reading the flag instead of the local also keeps it from
  subscribing to a turn it isn't following.
  `/ᐠ_ ꞈ _ᐟ\` is the private space's face, and it goes exactly where the
  ordinary one's `≽(◉˕◉≼マ` goes — same `PlaceholderBlock`, same "nothing to
  see here" — as the empty state AND as the tab switcher's backdrop, drawn as
  the switcher's first child so cards draw over it, off `privateMode` rather
  than off any card (it has to be there when the space is empty), and fading on
  `LocalPrivacy` so it arrives and leaves with the violet rather than being cut
  in a frame ahead of it. The caption is dropped in the private space (the
  `caption` parameter is nullable now, and null leaves the row out rather than
  rendering "", which would still take its gap and its line box and leave the
  face off-centre) — in there that line is the only thing on screen saying
  anything at all, which is the wrong note to end on.
  **The blur is progressive**, not a property of the card: a private page goes
  out of focus as it shrinks toward its switcher slot and comes back into focus
  as it is drawn out, tracking the drag. That's `privateShrinkBlur`, called
  from inside the same `graphicsLayer` block as `applyShrinkTransform` in BOTH
  `WebViewHost` and `AnimatedThumbnailHost` (the live element and its static
  stand-in must agree, or the handoff between them is a jump in focus) — so it
  is the live WebView that is blurred, which is the only option here, since the
  whole shrink is deliberately one element rather than a hand-off. It reads
  `scaleX` back off the transform and divides the radius by it, exactly like the
  corner radius does and for the same reason: a RenderEffect applies in the
  layer's own space, BEFORE the scale, so a fixed radius arrives on screen
  multiplied by however far the card has shrunk and lands visibly softer than
  the card it hands to. Linear in the shrink, not front-loaded like the mode
  turn's curve — the user is driving this one, and at its sharp end the page is
  fullscreen and they are looking straight at it anyway.
  At rest in the switcher it's `Modifier.privatePreview`, `PreviewBlur` at
  `BlurredEdgeTreatment.Rectangle` (Unbounded fades the edges and leaves a
  light frame inside the card's clip); that radius rides `LocalPrivacy` through
  a `sqrt`, deliberately front-loaded, since the way IN to private mode is
  420ms with a private page on screen at less than full blur. The two paths
  meet at exactly `PreviewBlur` on screen when the shrink lands.
  `PreviewBlur` is 20dp and was 36dp: because the shrink path asks for it
  divided by the scale, a comfortable sigma at card size is a punishing one at
  the far end of the drag, where RenderEffect's blur visibly degrades — it
  downsamples harder as sigma grows, and the level it picks changing frame to
  frame is what a slow drag showed as patches of the image pulsing in
  brightness. The other half of that fix is quantising the shrink radius to
  6px steps: the artifact is driven by the radius CHANGING, so holding it still
  for a run of frames holds the pattern still, while the movement the eye is
  actually following (the scale) stays perfectly smooth. Both paths read the
  one constant, so the two ends still agree exactly.
  Worth knowing for anything in this area: the live WebView is NOT what
  shrinks — `liveVisible` is `switchProgress == 0f`, so every intermediate
  state of every switcher gesture is already the static
  `AnimatedThumbnailHost`, and the blur is being applied to a bitmap.
  Note `Modifier.blur` and `renderEffect` are both RenderEffect, a silent no-op
  below API 31, and a no-op here means the page is shown in full — so below S
  the card's preview is dropped to `alpha(0f)` instead, outright rather than
  over 420ms (a fade would be 420ms of exactly the thing being hidden), and the
  shrink simply isn't blurred.
  **Leaving.** Settings > Behavior > "Keep private tabs" (persisted, default
  off) decides whether `setPrivateMode(false)` parks the private tabs or calls
  `closePrivateTabs`. That path destroys the WebViews and drops the rows only —
  there is no disk work to undo, since private tabs are already excluded from
  the saved tabs, the thumbnail store and the parked page states. The way in
  and out is the new-tab sheet's existing eye button, now hoisted so it drives
  the mode instead of a local `var` — and that is the only way in, deliberately:
  a duplicate switch in the menu was tried and removed. `privateMode` itself is
  never persisted — a relaunch always starts in
  the ordinary space.

- **Ad blocker: three switches, real lists, and cookie-banner hiding**
  (`core/BlocklistFeeds.kt`, `core/BlocklistStore.kt`, `core/CosmeticFilters.kt`,
  `ui/AdBlockScreen.kt`, all new; `AdBlocker`, `BrowserViewModel`, `MenuSheet`,
  `BrowserScreen`). The menu's Ad blocker **row** now navigates to a full-screen
  destination (the switch on it still toggles blocking in place); the screen is
  Block ads / Block trackers / Hide cookie banners, a rule count, Update now,
  Update weekly, and the user's own lists and rules. Deliberately not a catalog
  of interchangeable feeds — which lists are right is a judgement the app should
  make.
  **Lists.** Ads is HaGeZi Pro (`wildcard` + `-onlydomains`, 224,526 domains,
  rebuilt daily) — the most-used and most-maintained general DNS list, and the
  wildcard variant because it is compressed on the assumption the consumer does
  a suffix walk, which `AdBlocker` does. Trackers is AdGuard's CNAME-cloaked
  tracker list, which is a separate switch because it buys one specific thing an
  ordinary list structurally cannot: a tracker CNAMEd onto a subdomain of the
  site you're on looks first-party from every angle except the DNS record, so
  only an enumeration of the disguises works. Custom feed URLs run the same
  parser under `FeedRule.Auto` (sniffs hosts / bare-domain / `||domain^` per
  line).
  **Cookie banners are cosmetic, not network** — that's the whole reason they
  need their own machinery. A tracker is a request to somebody else's server and
  can be refused by hostname; a consent banner is markup the site itself renders
  from its own origin, so there is nothing to refuse. EasyList's cookie list
  (fanboy's cookiemonster) is 15,272 generic `##selector` rules and ~18k
  site-specific ones against just 180 usable domains, which is the shape of the
  problem. `CosmeticFilters` therefore builds a ~300KB stylesheet, injected at
  document start (before first paint, so no flash) and registered once per
  WebView the same way `applyDarkening` registers its script; site-specific
  rules are looked up by host walk and injected in `onPageStarted`, since a
  script registered once per WebView cannot know the host. Notes: CSS has no
  forgiving selector parsing outside `:is()`, so one selector the browser
  dislikes invalidates its whole rule — selectors are chunked 40 to a rule to
  bound that, extended pseudo-classes (`:has-text`, `:upward`, `:style`) are
  dropped since they are engine features rather than CSS, and rules with `~`
  exception domains or `#@#` are skipped whole rather than honored by halves.
  `FALLBACK_SELECTORS` covers the major consent platforms with no download at
  all, so the switch works before the first update and if one fails. The scroll
  lock a modal banner leaves behind is undone, but only when one of
  `MODAL_ROOTS` is actually in the document and only on the properties actually
  locked (read off `getComputedStyle`, not assumed) — blanket `overflow:auto`
  breaks real dialogs. Nothing is ever clicked on the user's behalf: consent is
  a legal declaration made in their name.
  **Storage.** Not in `BrowserStore`'s JSON blob — same reasoning as thumbnails.
  `filesDir/blocklist/` holds `hosts.txt` (merged, deduped, temp-then-renamed),
  `cosmetic_generic.css` + `cosmetic_specific.txt`, `config.json` and
  `meta.json`. A feed that fails is a failed row, not an aborted run; if *every*
  feed fails the previous files are left untouched.
  **`AdBlocker` holds a sorted `LongArray` of FNV-1a hashes**, not a
  `HashSet<String>`: 450k strings is tens of MB held for the life of the process
  where their hashes are 3.6MB, and the suffix walk hashes in place
  (`hash(host, from)`) rather than calling `substring` per label, so the hot path
  — a background thread, every subresource of every page — allocates nothing.
  The walk stops before the last label, since dotless rules are rejected at parse
  time.
  Own rules apply at `buildIndex` time, not download time, so they take effect
  without refetching (the field commits on an 800ms pause). Feed *toggles* can't:
  nothing is kept per feed, so they need a download, and the screen says so by
  diffing the subscribed set against what the last update fetched. Auto-update
  runs on launch when the list is over a week old, including the never-updated
  first launch.
  **Verified off-device** by compiling the real parser, matcher and cosmetic
  code out of the source with the embeddable Kotlin compiler and running them
  against the live feeds (`kotlin-compiler-embeddable` from the Gradle cache —
  needs `trove4j` and `kotlinx-coroutines` on the java classpath and
  `kotlin-stdlib` passed as `-classpath`). Results: HaGeZi parses to exactly its
  own header count; no hit on google/cloudflare/github/wikipedia/jsdelivr/paypal;
  ~210ns per lookup over a 450k index; all 15,272 generated selectors compile
  under a real CSS-selector engine (soupsieve); both injected scripts pass
  `node --check` with the full stylesheet embedded.

- **A relaunch resumes instead of reloading** (`core/PageStateStore.kt`, new;
  `BrowserViewModel.startupCoverTabId`, `StartupCover` in `BrowserScreen.kt`,
  `MainActivity.onStop`). Two halves of one thing. The **cover**: on a cold
  start the current tab's WebView has no page at all — it is created, told to
  load, and paints white until the network and the renderer are done — so the
  tab's saved thumbnail is held over it at full screen (same image the handoff
  cover uses, no enter transition, it's there from the first frame) until
  `onPageCommitVisible` says real pixels are up. That callback is the hinge:
  `onPageFinished` waits on subresources and is far too late. Also lifted by a
  main-frame load failure (there's a `WebErrorState` to show instead), by the
  load simply finishing (insurance against a missing commit callback), and at
  the outside by a 6s cap — a cover that outlives its page is a frozen app.
  `pageExposed` excludes it, or the cover would be captured as the tab's next
  preview. The **state**: each tab's `WebView.saveState` bundle is now written
  to `filesDir/pagestate/<id>.bin` at `onStop`/`onCleared` (exactly when
  thumbnails are flushed, and for the same reason), and `create` feeds it to
  `restoreState` instead of calling `loadUrl` — which is what makes the cover
  honest, since the still image is of a scrolled page and the live page now
  arrives at that same offset rather than jumping to the top. It also means
  back/forward history survives a restart. Notes: a `Bundle` is stored by
  marshalling a `Parcel`, which is explicitly not a durable format, so every
  file carries a format version *and* the WebView implementation's version and
  is discarded unread on any mismatch (a Play Store WebView update costs the
  parked states once, rather than feeding a foreign parcel to `restoreState`);
  the restored state is only used when its current item's URL matches the
  tab's, or an older park would put a different page on screen than the title
  and preview say; and the *current* tab's file is read synchronously in
  `restoreSavedState`, since its WebView is created by the first composition
  and can beat an IO job to it — the rest load on IO, with `pageStatesLoaded`
  gating a synchronous fallback in `create` for a tab opened inside that
  window.

- **Splash: neutral ground, accented cat** (`values/attrs.xml` new,
  `values*/colors.xml`, `values/themes.xml`, `drawable/splash_cat_frame_*.xml`,
  `splash_window.xml`, `MainActivity.followAccentOnSplash`). The splash ground
  is now plain white/near-black by the *system* light-dark setting at every API
  level — the API 31+ wallpaper-accent wash is gone — and the accent lives only
  in the kaomoji. The frames' tint is `?attr/splashInk` instead of
  `@color/splash_ink`, i.e. a theme attribute, which is the hinge: the app's
  accent setting can't be read at splash time (nothing is running), so it's
  baked into *which theme the splash is drawn with*.
  `Activity.getSplashScreen().setSplashScreenTheme(id)` (API 31+, the only
  handle there is) is fed a per-swatch `SplashTheme.*` — each one Theme.Browser
  with `splashInk` swapped, nine of them, colours mirroring
  `AccentTheme.color()` at tone 42 (72 under `values-night`). It registers the
  theme for the *next* splash, so a swatch change lands one launch later; the
  accent flow is collected rather than read once so that launch is right
  whenever it comes. `AccentTheme.Dynamic` passes `Resources.ID_NULL` (reset)
  and falls through to the default `@color/splash_ink`, which is the system
  accent on 31+ (`values-v31`) and a neutral below it. Pre-31 there is still no
  splash screen API, so `splash_window.xml` gets the same attribute but is
  fixed at the Dynamic default.

- **"Most visited" counts real visits** (`core/VisitTally.kt`, new;
  `BrowserViewModel`, `BrowserStore`, `NewTabSheet`). The ranking used to sort
  the 20-row recent-history list by a `visitCount` carried on those rows, so a
  page's total died with the row the moment it fell out of that recency window
  — a site opened daily came back as "1 visit" and the order looked arbitrary.
  Counts now live in a separate `VisitTally` table keyed by `visitKey(url)`
  (fragment and trailing slash dropped, scheme+host lowercased, query KEPT —
  for search results and web apps the query is the page), capped at
  `MAX_TALLIES` and pruned by *lowest count* rather than by age, since pruning
  by recency would just recreate the bug. `record()` still owns the redirect
  collapsing, and hands `countVisit` the URL it collapsed into so the
  pre-redirect leg's tally is folded into the settled URL instead of being
  stranded — and the visit is not counted twice, which the old
  `previous.visitCount + 1` did on every search-engine rewrite. History rows
  keep a copy of the count so the list still renders from one type. Tallies are
  persisted in the same JSON blob; an older save with no `visits` array is
  seeded from history's counts. Swipe-to-remove drops the tally too, or a
  deleted row would reappear ranked. Typed-query matching now searches history
  ∪ tallies (deduped by URL, history first so its live favicon wins).

- **Adaptive launcher icon** (`mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml`,
  `drawable/ic_launcher_foreground.xml`, `drawable-nodpi/ic_launcher_cat.png`,
  `tools/render_launcher_icon.swift`). `(•˕ •マ.ᐟ` on white, reversed (white on
  near-black) under `values-night`. The face is one PNG, solid black on
  transparent — its alpha is the shape, and `@color/ic_launcher_ink` is
  applied as a `<bitmap android:tint>`, so light/dark is a colour swap rather
  than two images to keep in step. Which one a launcher shows is decided by
  the configuration *it* loads the icon with, i.e. the system dark setting;
  the in-app `ThemeMode` can't reach it, since another process draws it.
  The glyph is baked the same way and for the same reason as the splash
  frames — katakana `マ` plus a Canadian syllabic `ᐟ`, i.e. per-glyph font
  fallback — and it is now sized and centred by its own INK rather than by
  its line box, the same way `render_splash_cats.swift` does it: a line box
  carries the font's full ascent and descent, so centring that leaves a face
  this short visibly high, and measuring the text's width alone leaves the
  fit to a constant that has to be re-guessed per face. The adaptive icon's
  inner-72-of-108dp safe zone is a CIRCLE, so what binds is the ink box's
  corner touching it — these faces are wide and short, so the diagonal runs
  out well before the width. It is fitted to 82% of that circle (`fill`)
  rather than to the whole of it: the safe zone is the guarantee of what
  SURVIVES the mask, not a target, and a face drawn out to it touches the
  edge of a round icon on both sides, which reads as cramped rather than as
  large. `android:gravity="fill"` on the bitmap, not
  `center`: launchers rasterise the icon at whatever size they like, and a
  centered bitmap would just sit there at its intrinsic size. A
  `<monochrome>` layer (Android 13+ themed icons) points at the same PNG
  *untinted* — the system tints that layer itself. `minSdk` is 26, so there's
  no legacy non-adaptive icon to maintain alongside it.
  Note this also gives the API 31+ splash a launcher icon to fall back to,
  which is what used to be the Android robot — the splash still overrides it
  with the cats explicitly.

- **Splash screen cycles cat kaomoji** (`values-v31/themes.xml`,
  `drawable/splash_cats.xml`, `drawable-nodpi/splash_cat_[0-7].png`,
  `MainActivity`). This is the *system* splash — the window that used to show
  the Android robot — not a Compose screen after it. The robot was there
  because the app declared no `android:icon` and the API 31+ splash falls back
  to the launcher icon; `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` now points at the
  cats instead.
  The icon is an `<animation-list>`, not an `AnimatedVectorDrawable`: a
  VectorDrawable can't render text, and the platform starts any splash icon
  that is `Animatable`, which `AnimationDrawable` is. Its frames are baked
  PNGs, not text drawn at runtime — these eight faces span Hangul, Sinhala,
  Kannada, Greek, Yi and more, i.e. per-glyph font fallback on whatever the
  device ships, so they're rendered once on macOS through CoreText (which
  does that fallback properly) by `tools/render_splash_cats.swift`.
  **Geometry** is the part that isn't obvious. The splash scales the icon into
  a *square* slot and masks it to a circle two thirds of that square across
  (the documented 240dp icon / 160dp visible safe zone), so the first attempt
  — tightly-cropped wide 512x141 frames — came out stretched vertically and
  clipped. Frames are square instead, and one point size is shared across the
  set (so the cats don't resize as they cycle), chosen by fitting the
  *tightest* face's ink box diagonally into that circle: these faces are wide
  and short, so the box's corner runs out of room well before its width does.
  Each frame is then centred by its **ink**, not its line box — a line box
  carries the font's full ascent and descent, and centring that leaves a short
  face visibly high. The renderer gets the ink box by drawing once and
  scanning the alpha, then redrawing shifted; deriving it from font metrics
  would need a special case per script.
  **Colour** comes from the theme, not from the PNGs: they're neutral grey
  with the shape in their alpha, and `@color/splash_ink` is applied as a
  tint. From API 31 that colour and `@color/splash_bg` are aliases for
  `@android:color/system_accent1_*` (`values-v31`, reversed in
  `values-night-v31`) — the same wallpaper-derived palette
  `AccentTheme.Dynamic` gives the app, so the splash matches the browser it
  opens into. A fixed accent swatch picked in Settings can't reach it, and
  neither can the in-app `ThemeMode`: the splash is drawn from resources
  alone, before any app state is read, so light/dark follows the *system*
  setting. Pre-31 falls back to the app's own neutrals (`emptyBg`/`inkMuted`).
  The tint is why each frame is a one-line `<bitmap>` wrapper
  (`splash_cat_frame_N.xml`): an `<animation-list>` item takes a drawable and
  a duration and nothing else.
  The splash is dismissed the instant the app's first frame lands, so
  `MainActivity.keepSplashUpWhileRestoring` holds it: an `OnPreDrawListener`
  that vetoes draws (what core-splashscreen's `setKeepOnScreenCondition` does,
  by hand rather than for one dependency) until the new
  `BrowserViewModel.startupComplete` — the saved JSON is read synchronously in
  `init`, but the thumbnails and favicons that go with it are IO jobs, so
  `restoreSavedState` now returns those `Job`s and the flag flips once they've
  joined. Bounded at 3s max (a held first frame reads as a hung launch) and
  nothing at the bottom: the splash goes the instant startup finishes, so on a
  fast start the cats just flash past — there was a 1.2s minimum hold, and it
  was removed deliberately. Skipped entirely when
  `startupComplete` is already true, i.e. on an Activity recreate, where the
  ViewModel outlived the Activity and there is no splash behind it to hold.
  Releasing needs an explicit `content.invalidate()`; the veto ate the pending
  draw request.
  Below API 31 there is no splash screen API at all, so the same first cat is
  the `android:windowBackground` (`drawable/splash_window.xml`), static — a
  window background is never started.

- **Grouped pile shows five cards, fanned** (`TabSwitcher.kt`). Long-press now
  reads as the centered card with a slightly smaller neighbour behind it on
  each side and a slightly smaller one again behind those, every one of them
  leaning away from the middle like a hand of cards (`STACK_TILT` +
  `STACK_TILT_STEP` per tier, plus a `STACK_TILT_JITTER` wobble hashed off the
  tab id in `Long.tiltNoise()` — deterministic, so a card keeps its own angle
  across regroupings instead of reshuffling on every recomposition). The lean
  is uniformly outward; a variant alternating it tier by tier (first tier's
  top corner out, next tier's bottom) was tried and looked worse.
  Two things were in the way of the extra tiers. They were capped by
  `STACK_MAX_DEPTH` on raw depth, but a **one-sided** pile (centered card at
  either end of the row — where it usually is, the current tab being last)
  alternates its cards left/right/left/right, so a cap of 2 dressed exactly
  one tier per side; the cap is now `STACK_SIDE_TIERS * 2` in that mode, and
  `pileScale` follows the *tier* rather than the depth, or the two halves of
  one tier come out different sizes. The real blocker was composition, not
  geometry: a `LazyRow` only composes what intersects its viewport, and at
  `CARD_WIDTH_FRACTION` that is the centered card ±1 — the outer cards did not
  exist to be drawn at any peek/scale. The row is therefore laid out
  `rowOverscan` (three slots) wider than the screen on each side via
  `requiredWidth`, with `sidePadding` bumped by the same amount so every card
  keeps its exact screen position, the scroll range is unchanged (content and
  viewport both grow by `2 * rowOverscan`) and `viewportCenter` — which
  `centeredTabId` and the snap both read — is still screen center. Notes:
  `Modifier.width` is coerced back into the incoming constraints and silently
  leaves the row screen-width (the padding then shoves every card off one
  side) — it must be `requiredWidth`; and do NOT add an offset to re-center
  it, since `Box` reports its child's full width without clamping to its own
  constraints, so the enclosing `Column`'s `CenterHorizontally` already hangs
  the overscan off both edges evenly.

- **Pull-to-refresh is content-aware, and toggleable**
  (`WebViewSwipeRefreshLayout` in `BrowserScreen.kt`, `pullToRefreshEnabled`
  through `BrowserStore`/`BrowserViewModel`/`BehaviorPane`). `canChildScrollUp`
  delegating to the WebView only knows the document's outermost scroll, which
  is wrong twice over: a page that scrolls inside an `overflow: auto` container
  (`body { overflow: hidden }` web apps, drawers, comment lists) reports "at the
  top" forever, so every downward flick reloaded instead of scrolling; and
  SwipeRefreshLayout looks only at a drag's vertical component, so a carousel or
  swipe menu died the moment the finger drifted a few degrees below horizontal.
  Three gates now sit in front of it: a **direction lock** (first movement past
  touch slop decides, latched for the gesture) and a **hit test in the page** on
  every touch-down — `elementFromPoint` (in CSS px, i.e. view px / `WebView.scale`,
  NOT offset by scrollX/Y, and stepping in/out of shadow roots) walking ancestors
  for one that is itself scrolled down or has claimed panning via `touch-action`.
  A page that owns a gesture without owning a scroller or setting `touch-action`
  (Google Images' swipe-to-close, bottom sheets, sliders) is caught by
  `core/PullGate.kt` (new): a document-start script wraps
  `EventTarget.addEventListener` and tags nodes that register a **non-passive**
  touchstart/touchmove — the spec's own "I intend to preventDefault" flag — which
  the ancestor walk then reads. Passive defaults differ by target, so listeners on
  window/document/body/documentElement count only with an explicit
  `passive: false`, or every site with an analytics touch handler on the document
  would lose the gesture entirely. Its second signal is a `@JavascriptInterface`
  callback fired when a touchmove actually comes back `defaultPrevented`; that one
  is retrospective, so it also drives a mid-pull bail — `super` is handed a
  synthesized ACTION_CANCEL, which settles the spinner without firing the refresh
  listener.
  The probe is async and is only ever read, never awaited — blocking touch
  dispatch on the renderer is the one stall this gesture can't afford — so a late
  answer just leaves the old behavior; a `gestureId` counter keeps the previous
  touch's answer from landing on the next one. When a gate says no, `super` is
  skipped entirely rather than having its verdict returned, or it accumulates
  drag distance and grabs the pointer as soon as the finger travels downward.

- **Downloads screen** (`core/DownloadsStore.kt`, `ui/DownloadsScreen.kt`,
  both new). The menu's Downloads row is a destination now, following the same
  full-screen pattern as Bookmarks/History (`ListScreenScaffold` and
  `EmptyListMessage` went `internal` for it). Saves land in the device's own
  `Downloads` folder — `MediaStore.Downloads` on API 29+, the public Download
  dir plus a media scan below that — not a private corner of the gallery. The
  list is NOT app state: it's an unfiltered query of that collection, which
  under scoped storage returns exactly this app's own contributions, re-read on
  every open and after every save, because the user can delete these files from
  any file manager and a list of our own would go stale silently.
  `deleteDownload` re-queries rather than dropping the row locally, so a
  refused delete leaves the row on screen. Each row decodes its own thumbnail
  (bounds pass first, then `inSampleSize`) and taps open the file through a
  chooser. "Show in file manager" is `DownloadManager.ACTION_VIEW_DOWNLOADS`:
  building a documents-provider folder URI and ACTION_VIEWing it looks
  equivalent and isn't — DocumentsUI's provider needs `MANAGE_DOCUMENTS`, so
  that threw SecurityException and took the app down with it.

- **Long-press context menu on links and images** (`core/WebContextTarget.kt`,
  `core/ImageSaver.kt`, `ui/WebContextMenu.kt`, all new). A long press that
  lands on a link offers Open / Open in new tab / Copy link; one that lands on
  a bare image (`IMAGE_TYPE`) offers Download image / Copy image. A LINKED
  image gets link actions only, deliberately: sites wrap images in anchors
  constantly (logos, icons before labels, whole cards), WebView reports every
  one of those as `SRC_IMAGE_ANCHOR_TYPE`, and image rows on what reads as an
  ordinary link is worse than the lost shortcut. Detection is `View.setOnLongClickListener` + `hitTestResult` in
  `BrowserViewModel.create` — returning `true` only for those hit types, so
  ordinary text still gets WebView's own selection ActionMode. Both anchor
  types go through `requestFocusNodeHref` (async, into a `Message`) because a
  linked image's `hitTestResult.extra` is the image src, never the href. A
  no-op `setOnTouchListener` records the finger's raw coordinates, the only
  place they exist, so the menu can be anchored to what was pressed. The menu
  is NOT a sheet — it's a full-screen overlay with a hand-placed `Layout` that
  clamps to the screen and flips above the finger near the bottom edge, drawn
  before the destinations (which dismiss it) and first in the `BackHandler`
  chain. `ImageSaver` re-requests the image with the page's cookies and
  `Referer` (WebView's cache isn't readable), decodes `data:` URIs in place,
  and says so plainly for `blob:`, which can't be reached from outside the
  renderer. `Base64.DEFAULT or Base64.URL_SAFE` is NOT "accept both" — URL_SAFE
  swaps the alphabet, so ORing them failed every ordinary data URI with "bad
  base-64"; standard first, url-safe as a fallback. Saves go to Downloads via MediaStore on API 29+ and a
  direct write plus media scan below it (`WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE`,
  `maxSdkVersion=28`, requested at the point of use); copy stages the bytes in
  `cacheDir/shared` and puts a `FileProvider` content URI on the clipboard, so
  it's the image that pastes, not its address. No haptic is fired when the
  menu opens: `View.performLongClick` already performs `LONG_PRESS` whenever a
  long-click listener handles the press, so doing it again from Compose reads
  as a double tick.

- **Haptics** (`ui/Haptics.kt`, new). One vocabulary — `tap`, `toggle(on)`,
  `longPress`, `tick`, `gestureStart`/`gestureEnd`, `confirm`, `reject` — used
  by every control. Goes through `View.performHapticFeedback` with platform
  `HapticFeedbackConstants`, not Compose's `LocalHapticFeedback`, which in this
  Compose version only knows `LongPress`/`TextHandleMove`; effects newer than
  API 26 fall back to the closest older constant. Nothing passes
  `FLAG_IGNORE_GLOBAL_SETTING`, so the system's touch-feedback setting still
  wins. Deliberately NOT everywhere: ordinary navigation is silent — links and
  history/bookmark/suggestion rows, the address bar and its Go, the menu
  button, back/forward/copy/reload, menu and settings rows, pane back buttons,
  theme/sort/engine pickers. What buzzes is state changes and gestures.
  Wiring is concentrated in the shared components (`MenuRow`, its new
  `MenuSwitch`, `Tile`) rather than at each call site; `MenuRow`/`Tile` take
  the post-tap state (`toggledTo`/`toggles`) so a switch row feels the same
  whether the row or the switch itself was hit, and a tile that only navigates
  stays silent. Gesture-wise: the tabs-button drag and each card's close/expand drag
  fire `gestureStart` at the moment they clear their dead zone and
  `gestureEnd` on release, with `confirm` reserved for what destroys or
  commits (closing a tab, close-all, removing a bookmark/history entry,
  pull-to-refresh) and `reject` for a drag released without committing. Scrolling the switcher
  ticks once per tab passed — a `snapshotFlow` on `centeredTabId` gated on
  `listState.isScrollInProgress`, so closing a tab or opening the switcher on
  a different one doesn't tick.
- **Tabs-button drag anchors the card to the finger** (`BottomToolbar`,
  `BrowserScreen`). The button reports the finger's window position when the
  drag commits; `dragAnchorCorrection` then moves the shrinking page so the
  card's bottom-left corner lands under the finger instead of leaving the
  page's center where it was. Scaled by shrink progress, so it's exactly zero
  at fullscreen (no jump when the drag is recognized) and exact once the card
  reaches its final size. Card size comes from `targetRect` with the same
  `FALLBACK_SHRINK_SCALE` fallback `applyShrinkTransform` uses, so the anchor
  can never disagree with the scale being drawn.

- **Previews are copied, not redrawn; page laid out above the toolbar**
  (`BrowserViewModel`, `BrowserScreen`, `TabSwitcher`). `View.draw(Canvas)`
  asks Chromium to re-render the page through a path it never uses for
  display, and anything in its own compositor layer (vector/SVG content,
  transforms, fixed/sticky, filters) came back missing or at the wrong
  size/place. The tab on screen is now captured with
  `PixelCopy.request(Window, …)` over the WebView's window rect — literally
  the pixels the user is looking at. Notes:
  - The Activity's `Window` must be found by walking the WebView's **parent
    chain** (`hostWindow()`): the WebViews are created with the Application
    context, and `rootView.context` is a `DecorContext` whose base is also the
    application context, so unwrapping that dead-ends.
  - The copy lands a frame or two after it's asked for and is applied
    whenever it does — a copy is never skipped because "nothing changed", since
    a page can repaint itself with nothing to announce it (a clock, a feed).
    `exactCaptureAt` vs `contentChangedAt` only stops a *software* draw from
    replacing a good copy, which is what leaves a backgrounded tab showing the
    exact picture from when it was last on screen. `notePageMoved` (scroll,
    load, progress) also queues a capture on a 250 ms settle.
  - `setPageExposed` is pushed down from `BrowserScreen` — only Compose knows
    whether a sheet, cover, destination or shrink transform is over the page,
    and a window copy is only a copy of the page when nothing is.
  - The page is laid out **above** the toolbar (`pageBottomInset` padding on
    the live element's wrapper Box, following `toolbarVisible`), so a site's
    own bottom bar/buttons aren't stuck under it and the copy is all page.
    `WebView.setPadding` is useless for this — Chromium ignores it entirely
    (verified with an 800px padding); only layout size reaches the viewport.
    That Box's `PageBg` background must stay conditional on `liveVisible`: it
    covers the whole screen and would otherwise paint over the switcher.
    `TabSwitcher`'s card aspect is now plain `maxHeight / maxWidth`, since the
    page and the switcher's box are both screen-minus-toolbar.
  - Everything that positions the shrinking page measures from the PAGE's
    center, not `boxCoordinates`' center — `pageCenterY()`, used by
    `targetOffset`/`targetOffsetFor`, and `FloatingTabLabel`'s `fullHeightPx`.
    The inset is hoisted into `pageBottomInsetPx` (published by a `SideEffect`
    from the Scaffold content, the only place the bar's height is known)
    because those live above it. Miss one and the page animates half an inset
    above the card it settles into.

- **Long-press to group tabs** (`TabSwitcher.kt`). A long press on any card
  gathers every tab into a single pile under the centered one (neighbors slide
  in from both sides, fanned by `STACK_PEEK` per step of depth and stacked by
  `zIndex`), and one swipe up on that pile clears them all. The `stacked` flag
  lives inside `TabSwitcher` — nothing outside the grid needs it — with its own
  `BackHandler` (registered inside the grid, so it ungroups before
  BrowserScreen's chain closes the switcher). While gathered, the pile is
  dragged as one object via `stackDragY` on the row's `Column`, so `TabCard`
  suppresses its own `translationY`; cards behind the front one have both
  pointer input and `combinedClickable` disabled, because their layout slots
  never move (only their drawing does) and taps out at the row's edges would
  otherwise hit a card drawn in the middle.

- **Favicons persist too** (`core/FaviconStore.kt`, new). One PNG per host
  (PNG, not WEBP — lossy artifacts show on a 64px icon) under
  `filesDir/favicons/`, keyed by host because that's the key a tab, a history
  row, a bookmark and `Favicons`' network fallback all share. `Favicons` now
  reads disk before going to the network and writes what it fetches, so it's
  attached once via `Favicons.attach(faviconStore)` from `BrowserViewModel`.
  `onReceivedIcon` also seeds the shared memory cache (`Favicons.remember`)
  and writes to disk for non-private tabs. Restore hydrates tab + history
  bitmaps (only where still `null`) and prunes files for hosts nothing refers
  to any more.
- **No thumbnail flash at startup** (`BrowserScreen.kt`). The `handoffCover`
  effect now only fires once the live page has actually been away
  (`handoffArmed`, set when `liveVisible` goes false). It fired on the first
  composition too, which was harmless while thumbnails died with the process
  but, now that they're restored, flashed the previous session's picture of
  the page fullscreen for a few frames before the real load appeared.
- **Tab previews persist across restarts** (`core/ThumbnailStore.kt`, new).
  Thumbnails are the one piece of state that doesn't live in `BrowserStore`'s
  JSON blob — they're far bigger than everything else combined and are
  disposable — so they get one WEBP file per tab id under
  `filesDir/thumbnails/`, written temp-file-then-renamed so a kill mid-write
  can't leave a corrupt preview. `captureThumbnail` queues the bitmap into
  `pendingThumbnails`, flushed on a 500 ms-debounced flow on `Dispatchers.IO`
  (same shape as the existing `dirty`/`persistNow` pair) so one switcher open
  = one encode per tab, not one per re-drag. Private tabs are never written,
  matching `BrowserStore` excluding them from the saved tab list. Restore
  prunes files for tabs that no longer exist, then fills each tab's
  `thumbnail` only if it's still `null`, so a live capture that beat the disk
  read wins. `MainActivity.onStop` calls `captureAndFlushThumbnails()` (skips
  the debounce) so a restored session shows the page the user was actually
  looking at, not whatever the switcher last captured.
- **Tab switcher: expand the centered card, not the stale current tab**
  (`TabSwitcher.kt`, `BrowserScreen.kt`). In grid ("Vertical") mode, tapping
  the tabs button to close the switcher now expands whichever card is
  centered in the row at that moment, not whichever tab was `currentId`
  before the row was scrolled — `TabSwitcher` reports the centered card's id
  + `LayoutCoordinates` via a new `onCenteredCardPositioned` callback
  (mirrors the existing `onCurrentCardPositioned`), and `BrowserScreen`'s
  `onTabs` handler feeds that into the same `selectTabFromCard` path a
  direct card tap already used, instead of the old blind `closeSwitcher()`.
  Also fixed a reorder-jump bug this surfaced in `selectTabFromCard` itself:
  it was calling `vm.selectTab(id)` (which moves the tab to the end of the
  list) *before* the zoom-to-fullscreen animation finished, and the grid
  stays mounted for that whole animation (`switcherOpen || switchProgress >
  0f`), so surrounding cards visibly reflowed mid-zoom. `vm.selectTab` is
  now called only after `progress.animateTo(0f, …)` completes, once the grid
  has actually unmounted.
- **Rename**: package renamed `com.kiyotaka.browser` → `com.yuku.browser`
  throughout (source tree moved, `build.gradle.kts` namespace/applicationId,
  README references). No functional change.
- **Empty state (`EmptyState.kt`)**: "404" now renders in Bitcount Grid
  Double at weight 255, ~210sp (fills most of the screen), tight negative
  spacing to the "nothing to see here" caption (monospace). Both use
  `EmptyGlyph` so they stay barely-visible in both light and dark.
- **Bookmarks & History**: real screens (`ui/ListScreens.kt`,
  `BookmarksScreen`/`HistoryScreen`), reached from `MenuSheet`'s rows,
  following the full-screen destination pattern above. `BrowserViewModel`
  gained `bookmarks: List<BookmarkEntry>` keyed by URL (not tab id) so the
  Bookmark quick-toggle's active state reflects whether the *current tab's
  URL* is bookmarked, not a per-tab flag.
- **Quick-toggle tiles** (`MenuSheet.Tile`): four independent rounded squares
  (not one merged strip), icon+label centered as one block (26dp icon), tonal
  accent wash when active. Dark/Desktop/Bookmark tiles swap between filled
  and outlined icon variants based on on/off state.
- **Unified omnibox look**: search bar (`NewTabSheet`) and address bar
  (`MenuSheet`) share the same pill shape + drop shadow. Tapping the address
  hands the sheet over to `NewTabSheet` — see the address-edit entry at the
  top of this list; it used to grow a `BasicTextField` in place instead.
- **App theming revamp**: Material You dynamic color (`AccentTheme.Dynamic`,
  default) via `dynamicLightColorScheme`/`dynamicDarkColorScheme` on API 31+,
  with fixed-palette "Android suggested colors" swatches as an alternative,
  and an empty "Special themes" section reserved for future use. Real
  System/Light/Dark `ThemeMode` (not just per-page WebView darkening as
  before — that was previously a no-op bug since the app theme never actually
  went dark). Status/nav bar icon contrast now follows the resolved theme via
  `WindowCompat`.
- **App settings**: full-screen page (`SettingsSheet.kt`, `SettingsScreen` +
  `SettingsPane`) with drill-down navigation (Root → Search engine /
  Appearance), replacing the old single-sheet settings.
- **Toolbar polish**: tab-switcher-mode icon (3 lines, rotates
  vertical↔horizontal) resized down to visually match the "+"/chevron icons;
  loading-line progress indicator now an overlay (doesn't reserve layout
  height); toolbar hides on scroll-down, reappears on scroll-up.
